/doi.org/10.1021/ja010890y"]]}],"x":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Reacting?","a":"Do the highlighted atoms bond at 20 °C, within minutes to hours?","o":[{"x":"Yes, they react"},{"x":"No, too slow"}],"y":1,"fig":{"pair":[2529,2628],"names":["Phenylsilane in CH₂Cl₂","Fumaronitrile"]},"ex":"N + E = 0.06 + (−15.71) = −15.65, below −5: the pair is too slow at 20 °C. log k = sN(N + E) = 0.71 × −15.65 = −11.1 (k ≈ 8 × 10⁻¹² M⁻¹ s⁻¹).","lk":[{"h":"Phenylsilane","l":[["Mayr database entry 58","https://www.cup.lmu.de/oc/mayr/reaktionsdatenbank/fe/details/58"],["Chem. Eur. J. 2013, 19, 249-263","https://doi.org/10.1002/chem.201202839"]]},{"h":"Fumaronitrile","l":[["Mayr database entry 1196","https://www.cup.lmu.de/oc/mayr/reaktionsdatenbank/fe/details/1196"],["Eur. J. Org. Chem. 2014, , 2956-2963","https://doi.org/10.1002/ejoc.201301779"]]}],"x":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks CH₃I, THF?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"α-carbon"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"oxygen"}],"t":"Acetone enolate","p":"With CH₃I, THF","fig":{"sites":2663},"ex":"Alkyl halides react at carbon. SN2 has a high intrinsic barrier, so the more stable product wins: C-alkylation keeps the strong C=O bond, O-alkylation would give a much less stable enol ether."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks Me₃SiCl, THF?","o":[{"pre":"O atom","x":"oxygen"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"α-carbon"}],"t":"Acetone enolate","p":"With Me₃SiCl, THF","fig":{"sites":2664},"ex":"Silicon forms a very strong bond to oxygen, so silyl chlorides trap enolates on O: this is how silyl enol ethers are made."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks methyl vinyl ketone?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"β-carbon"},{"pre":"N atom","x":"nitrogen"}],"t":"1-Pyrrolidinocyclohexene","p":"With methyl vinyl ketone","fig":{"sites":2665},"ex":"Stork enamine reaction: attack at nitrogen is reversible, attack at the β-carbon gives a stable C–C bond. Mayr's N = 14.91 for this enamine describes the carbon."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks an electrophile (E⁺)?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C3"},{"pre":"N atom","x":"N1"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2"}],"t":"Indole","p":"With an electrophile (E⁺)","fig":{"sites":2666},"ex":"At C3 the nitrogen stabilises the cation and the benzene ring stays intact."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks an electrophile (E⁺)?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C3"},{"pre":"N atom","x":"N1"}],"t":"Pyrrole","p":"With an electrophile (E⁺)","fig":{"sites":2667},"ex":"Attack at C2 gives a cation shared over three atoms, including N; attack at C3 shares it over only two."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks Ph₂CH⁺ (a benzhydrylium ion)?","o":[{"pre":"S atom","x":"sulfur"},{"pre":"N atom","x":"nitrogen"}],"t":"Thiocyanate","p":"With Ph₂CH⁺ (a benzhydrylium ion)","fig":{"sites":2668},"ex":"Sulfur attacks faster (N = 17.9 for S, about 12 for N in MeCN). The more stable isothiocyanates, from N-attack, form only when the thiocyanate can come off and add again (thermodynamic control)."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks CH₃I?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"carbon"},{"pre":"N atom","x":"nitrogen"}],"t":"Cyanide","p":"With CH₃I","fig":{"sites":2669},"ex":"Nitriles form. Isocyanides appear only with diffusion-controlled carbocations or with silver cyanide, where Ag⁺ blocks the carbon end."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks CH₃I, DMF?","o":[{"pre":"O atom","x":"oxygen"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ortho carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"para carbon"}],"t":"Phenoxide","p":"With CH₃I, DMF","fig":{"sites":2670},"ex":"Williamson ether synthesis: O-alkylation leaves the ring aromatic; attack by a ring carbon would first break the aromaticity."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks acetic anhydride?","o":[{"pre":"N atom","x":"nitrogen"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"para carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ortho carbon"}],"t":"Aniline","p":"With acetic anhydride","fig":{"sites":2671},"ex":"The amine nitrogen is the nucleophile towards acylating agents; the ring only reacts with strong electrophiles such as Br₂."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks acetic anhydride?","o":[{"pre":"N atom","x":"amine nitrogen"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"phenol oxygen"}],"t":"4-Aminophenol","p":"With acetic anhydride","fig":{"sites":2672},"ex":"This is how paracetamol is made: the amine is the better nucleophile, so acetylation stops at the amide."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks Boc₂O?","o":[{"pre":"N atom","x":"nitrogen"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"oxygen"}],"t":"Ethanolamine","p":"With Boc₂O","fig":{"sites":2673},"ex":"Amines are much better nucleophiles than alcohols (Mayr N = 14.1 for ethanolamine's nitrogen in MeCN, against about 7 for an alcohol)."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks iodoacetamide, pH 8?","o":[{"pre":"S atom","x":"thiolate sulfur"},{"pre":"N atom","x":"amine nitrogen"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"carboxylate oxygen"}],"t":"L-Cysteinate","p":"With iodoacetamide, pH 8","fig":{"sites":2674},"ex":"Thiolates are far more nucleophilic than amines: N = 23.4 for cysteine's thiolate against 13.5 for glycinate's amine, both in water."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks an electrophile (E⁺)?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"terminal CH₂ (γ)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"middle CH (β)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"CH₂ next to Si (α)"}],"t":"Allyltrimethylsilane","p":"With an electrophile (E⁺)","fig":{"sites":2675},"ex":"Attack at the far end puts the positive charge β to silicon, where the C–Si bond stabilises it; then the silyl group leaves."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks benzaldehyde, TiCl₄?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"α-carbon"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"oxygen"}],"t":"1-(Trimethylsiloxy)cyclohexene","p":"With benzaldehyde, TiCl₄","fig":{"sites":2676},"ex":"Mukaiyama aldol reaction: the silyl enol ether attacks with its α-carbon."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks an electrophile (E⁺)?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"terminal CH₂"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"CH next to O"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"oxygen"}],"t":"Ethyl vinyl ether","p":"With an electrophile (E⁺)","fig":{"sites":2677},"ex":"Attack at the terminal CH₂ leaves the positive charge next to oxygen, which stabilises it (an oxocarbenium ion)."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks acetic anhydride?","o":[{"pre":"N atom","x":"ring nitrogen"},{"pre":"N atom","x":"NMe₂ nitrogen"}],"t":"DMAP","p":"With acetic anhydride","fig":{"sites":2678},"ex":"The NMe₂ lone pair is delocalised into the ring and onto the ring nitrogen, which attacks and forms the N-acetylpyridinium ion."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks CH₃I?","o":[{"pre":"N atom","x":"N3 (pyridine-like)"},{"pre":"N atom","x":"N1 (N–H)"}],"t":"Imidazole","p":"With CH₃I","fig":{"sites":2679},"ex":"The lone pair of the N–H nitrogen is part of the aromatic sextet; the other nitrogen's lone pair is free."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks an electrophile (E⁺)?","o":[{"pre":"N atom","x":"imine nitrogen"},{"pre":"N atom","x":"amine nitrogen"}],"t":"DBU","p":"With an electrophile (E⁺)","fig":{"sites":2680},"ex":"Amidines react at the sp² nitrogen: the cation formed is shared by both nitrogens."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks acetone?","o":[{"pre":"N atom","x":"nitrogen"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"oxygen"}],"t":"Hydroxylamine","p":"With acetone","fig":{"sites":2681},"ex":"Nitrogen attacks, so carbonyl compounds give oximes."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks BnBr (1 equiv)?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"terminal CH₂ (γ)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"central CH (α)"}],"t":"Ethyl acetoacetate dianion","p":"With BnBr (1 equiv)","fig":{"sites":2682},"ex":"The second proton comes off the less acidic CH₃. That carbanion is the more basic, less stabilised one, so it reacts first (Weiler alkylation).","h":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom attacks CH₃I, NaH, DMF?","o":[{"pre":"N atom","x":"nitrogen"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"oxygen"}],"t":"2-Pyridone anion","p":"With CH₃I, NaH, DMF","fig":{"sites":2683},"ex":"Alkali salts give mainly N-methyl-2-pyridone, which keeps a strong C=O bond; silver salts give the O-alkylated 2-alkoxypyridine.","h":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does (CH₃)₂CuLi attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"β-carbon (C3)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"carbonyl carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"α-carbon (C2)"}],"t":"Cyclohexenone","p":"With (CH₃)₂CuLi","fig":{"sites":2684},"ex":"Cuprates add 1,4 (conjugate addition)."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does CH₃Li attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"carbonyl carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"β-carbon (C3)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"α-carbon (C2)"}],"t":"Cyclohexenone","p":"With CH₃Li","fig":{"sites":2685},"ex":"Organolithiums add fast and irreversibly to the carbonyl carbon (1,2-addition)."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does diethyl malonate, NaOEt attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"β-carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"carbonyl carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"α-carbon"}],"t":"Methyl vinyl ketone","p":"With diethyl malonate, NaOEt","fig":{"sites":2686},"ex":"Stabilised enolates add 1,4 (Michael addition): addition to the C=O is reversible, the 1,4-adduct is not."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does benzylamine attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"β-carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"carbonyl carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"α-carbon"}],"t":"Ethyl acrylate","p":"With benzylamine","fig":{"sites":2687},"ex":"Aza-Michael addition at room temperature; aminolysis of the ester is much slower."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does NaBH₄ (1 equiv), 0 °C attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"aldehyde carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ester carbon"}],"t":"Methyl 4-formylbenzoate","p":"With NaBH₄ (1 equiv), 0 °C","fig":{"sites":2688},"ex":"Aldehydes are much more electrophilic than esters, whose C=O is stabilised by the OMe lone pair."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does NaBH₄ attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"ketone carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ester carbon"}],"t":"Methyl levulinate","p":"With NaBH₄","fig":{"sites":2689},"ex":"NaBH₄ reduces ketones but esters only very slowly."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does morpholine attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"acyl chloride carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ester carbon"}],"t":"Methyl 4-(chlorocarbonyl)benzoate","p":"With morpholine","fig":{"sites":2690},"ex":"Acid chlorides are the most reactive acyl derivatives: Cl donates little to the C=O and leaves easily."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does an amine, 0 °C attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"acyl carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"CH₂Cl carbon"}],"t":"Chloroacetyl chloride","p":"With an amine, 0 °C","fig":{"sites":2691},"ex":"Addition to the acyl chloride C=O is far faster than SN2 at CH₂Cl, so chloroacetamides form."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does morpholine attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"CH₂Br carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"carbonyl carbon"}],"t":"Phenacyl bromide","p":"With morpholine","fig":{"sites":2692},"ex":"Amines add to ketones reversibly; SN2 next to a C=O is unusually fast, so the α-amino ketone forms."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does an amine attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"formyl carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"acetyl carbon"}],"t":"Acetic formic anhydride","p":"With an amine","fig":{"sites":2693},"ex":"Formylation, not acetylation: the formyl carbon has no methyl group to donate electrons or block the approach."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does an amine attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"central carbon"},{"pre":"N atom","x":"nitrogen"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"oxygen"}],"t":"Phenyl isocyanate","p":"With an amine","fig":{"sites":2694},"ex":"Amines add to the central carbon, giving ureas."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does an amine attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"thioester carbon"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ester carbon"}],"t":"Ethyl 4-(ethylsulfanyl)-4-oxobutanoate","p":"With an amine","fig":{"sites":2695},"ex":"Thioesters are activated acyl groups: sulfur donates less to the C=O than oxygen and thiolate leaves easily. Nature uses them in acetyl-CoA, and chemists in native chemical ligation."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does NaCN, DMSO attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"benzylic CH₂Br"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"aryl C–Br"}],"t":"4-Bromobenzyl bromide","p":"With NaCN, DMSO","fig":{"sites":2696},"ex":"SN2 is impossible at an sp² aryl carbon; at a benzylic carbon it is fast."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does NaN₃ (1 equiv) attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"primary CH₂–OMs"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"secondary CH–OMs"}],"t":"Butane-1,3-diyl dimesylate","p":"With NaN₃ (1 equiv)","fig":{"sites":2697},"ex":"SN2 is much faster at a primary carbon than at a secondary one."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does an amino acid's NH₂ attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C1 (C–F)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2 (C–NO₂)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C4 (C–NO₂)"}],"t":"1-Fluoro-2,4-dinitrobenzene (Sanger's reagent)","p":"With an amino acid's NH₂","fig":{"sites":2698},"ex":"SNAr: the nitro groups ortho and para to C1 hold the negative charge of the Meisenheimer complex, then fluoride leaves."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does a carbanion attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"exocyclic CH"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"carbonyl carbon"}],"t":"4-Benzylidene-2,6-di-tert-butylcyclohexa-2,5-dienone","p":"With a carbanion","fig":{"sites":2699},"ex":"Attack at the exocyclic carbon turns the quinone methide into an aromatic phenolate."},{"s":"rl","k":"Most reactive atom","a":"Which labelled atom does a phenoxide attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"epoxide CH₂"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"epoxide CH"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"CH₂Cl"}],"t":"Epichlorohydrin","p":"With a phenoxide","fig":{"sites":2700},"ex":"Most nucleophiles open the strained epoxide at its less hindered carbon; the alkoxide formed then displaces chloride and closes a new epoxide.","h":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"para"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ortho"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"meta"}],"t":"Anisole","p":"Reagents: Br₂, AcOH","fig":{"sites":2701},"ex":"OMe donates into the ring, so ortho and para attack give a cation stabilised on oxygen. Para wins: it is less crowded."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"meta"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ortho"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"para"}],"t":"Nitrobenzene","p":"Reagents: HNO₃, H₂SO₄","fig":{"sites":2702},"ex":"Ortho and para attack would put the positive charge next to the electron-poor nitro group; meta attack avoids that."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"meta"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ortho"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"para"}],"t":"Benzaldehyde","p":"Reagents: HNO₃, H₂SO₄","fig":{"sites":2703},"ex":"Like NO₂, the CHO group withdraws electrons and directs meta."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"para"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ortho"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"meta"}],"t":"Acetanilide","p":"Reagents: Br₂, AcOH","fig":{"sites":2704},"ex":"The amide nitrogen donates into the ring; the bulky NHAc group shields the ortho positions."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"para"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ortho"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"meta"}],"t":"tert-Butylbenzene","p":"Reagents: HNO₃, H₂SO₄","fig":{"sites":2705},"ex":"Alkyl groups direct ortho and para; the bulky tert-butyl group blocks ortho."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"para"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ortho"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"meta"}],"t":"Chlorobenzene","p":"Reagents: HNO₃, H₂SO₄","fig":{"sites":2706},"ex":"Halogens slow the reaction down but direct ortho and para: their lone pairs stabilise the cations formed there."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"para"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ortho"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"meta"}],"t":"Toluene","p":"Reagents: CH₃COCl, AlCl₃","fig":{"sites":2707},"ex":"Friedel–Crafts acylation is almost entirely para: the acylium–AlCl₄⁻ electrophile is bulky."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"ortho to OMe"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ortho to CH₃"}],"t":"4-Methylanisole","p":"Reagents: Br₂, AcOH","fig":{"sites":2708},"ex":"When two groups compete, the stronger donor decides: OMe beats CH₃."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"ortho to CH₃"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"ortho to NO₂"}],"t":"4-Nitrotoluene","p":"Reagents: Br₂, FeBr₃","fig":{"sites":2709},"ex":"Both groups agree: CH₃ directs ortho to itself and NO₂ directs meta to itself, which is the same carbon."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C3"},{"pre":"N atom","x":"N1"}],"t":"Pyrrole","p":"Reagents: POCl₃, DMF (Vilsmeier)","fig":{"sites":2667},"ex":"Attack at C2 gives a cation shared over three atoms, including N; attack at C3 shares it over only two."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C3"},{"pre":"N atom","x":"N1"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2"}],"t":"Indole","p":"Reagents: POCl₃, DMF (Vilsmeier)","fig":{"sites":2666},"ex":"At C3 the nitrogen stabilises the cation without disrupting the benzene ring."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C3"}],"t":"Furan","p":"Reagents: Ac₂O, BF₃·OEt₂","fig":{"sites":2710},"ex":"As with pyrrole: the cation from C2 attack is shared over three atoms."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C3"}],"t":"Thiophene","p":"Reagents: CH₃COCl, SnCl₄","fig":{"sites":2711},"ex":"As with furan and pyrrole, attack at C2 gives a cation shared over three atoms, one of them sulfur."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C3"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C4"}],"t":"Pyridine","p":"Reagents: Br₂, oleum, 130 °C","fig":{"sites":2712},"ex":"Pyridine reacts only under forcing conditions, at C3: the one position where the cation never puts positive charge on nitrogen."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C4"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C3"}],"t":"Pyridine N-oxide","p":"Reagents: HNO₃, H₂SO₄","fig":{"sites":2713},"ex":"The N-oxide oxygen donates into the ring and directs to C4. PCl₃ removes the oxygen afterwards."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does the amide ion attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C4"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C3"}],"t":"Pyridine","p":"Reagents: NaNH₂, then H₂O (Chichibabin)","fig":{"sites":2714},"ex":"Nucleophiles add at C2 or C4, where the negative charge of the adduct can sit on nitrogen; the amide ion goes to C2."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C1 (α)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2 (β)"}],"t":"Naphthalene","p":"Reagents: HNO₃, H₂SO₄","fig":{"sites":2715},"ex":"The cation from C1 attack has more resonance structures that keep the other ring fully aromatic."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2 (β)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C1 (α)"}],"t":"Naphthalene","p":"Reagents: H₂SO₄, 160 °C","fig":{"sites":2716},"ex":"Sulfonation is reversible. At 80 °C the faster 1-sulfonic acid forms (kinetic control); at 160 °C the more stable 2-isomer accumulates, free of the clash with the H at C8 (thermodynamic control).","h":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does it react?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C1"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C3"}],"t":"2-Methoxynaphthalene","p":"Reagents: Br₂, AcOH","fig":{"sites":2717},"ex":"C1 is both ortho to OMe and an α-position, so attack there keeps the other ring aromatic."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which chlorine is replaced first?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C4"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2"}],"t":"2,4-Dichloropyrimidine","p":"Reagents: morpholine, Et₃N","fig":{"sites":2718},"ex":"SNAr at C4 is usually faster, though often with some C2 product, and substituents on the ring can reverse it.","h":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2719},{"m":2720}],"t":"Propene","fig":{"r":[2721],"cond":["HBr"]},"ex":"Markovnikov: H⁺ adds to CH₂ to give the more stable secondary cation, and bromide adds there."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2722},{"m":2723}],"t":"Styrene","fig":{"r":[2724],"cond":["HBr"]},"ex":"The benzylic cation is far more stable than a primary one."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2725},{"m":2726},{"m":2727}],"t":"1-Methylcyclohexene","fig":{"r":[2728],"cond":["1. BH₃·THF  2. H₂O₂, NaOH"]},"ex":"Boron goes to the less substituted carbon and H to the other, both from the same face (syn addition). Oxidation keeps the configuration, so OH and CH₃ end up trans (racemic; one enantiomer drawn)."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2729},{"m":2730}],"t":"1-Hexene","fig":{"r":[2731],"cond":["1. Hg(OAc)₂, H₂O  2. NaBH₄"]},"ex":"Oxymercuration follows Markovnikov's rule without rearrangement: water opens the mercurinium ion at the more substituted carbon."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2732},{"m":2733}],"t":"1-Hexyne","fig":{"r":[2734],"cond":["H₂O, H₂SO₄, HgSO₄"]},"ex":"Markovnikov hydration gives the enol of the methyl ketone, which tautomerises."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2733},{"m":2732}],"t":"1-Hexyne","fig":{"r":[2734],"cond":["1. 9-BBN  2. H₂O₂, NaOH"]},"ex":"Anti-Markovnikov: boron goes to the terminal carbon, and the enol formed on oxidation tautomerises to the aldehyde."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2735},{"m":2736}],"t":"Propene","fig":{"r":[2721],"cond":["Br₂, H₂O"]},"ex":"Water opens the bromonium ion at the carbon that carries more of the positive charge, the more substituted one."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2737},{"m":2738},{"m":2739}],"t":"3,3-Dimethylbut-1-ene","fig":{"r":[2740],"cond":["HCl"]},"ex":"The secondary cation rearranges by a methyl shift to a tertiary cation before chloride adds.","h":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2741},{"m":2742}],"t":"Buta-1,3-diene","fig":{"r":[2743],"cond":["HBr, −80 °C"]},"ex":"At low temperature the faster 1,2-addition wins: bromide adds next to where the proton went (kinetic control).","h":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2742},{"m":2741}],"t":"Buta-1,3-diene","fig":{"r":[2743],"cond":["HBr, 40 °C"]},"ex":"When warm, the addition is reversible and the more stable 1,4-product, with the more substituted C=C, takes over (thermodynamic control).","h":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2744},{"m":2745}],"t":"Isoprene + methyl acrylate","fig":{"r":[2746],"cond":["heat (Diels–Alder)"]},"ex":"The 'para' rule: a 2-substituted diene puts the two substituents 1,4 in the ring.","h":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2747},{"m":2748}],"t":"(E)-1-Methoxybuta-1,3-diene + methyl acrylate","fig":{"r":[2749],"cond":["heat (Diels–Alder)"]},"ex":"The 'ortho' rule: a 1-substituted diene puts the substituents on neighbouring carbons. The new bond forms first between the diene carbon far from OMe and the acrylate CH₂, where the orbital coefficients are largest.","h":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which α-carbon forms the enolate?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C6 (CH₂)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2 (CHCH₃)"}],"t":"2-Methylcyclohexanone","p":"Reagents: LDA, THF, −78 °C","fig":{"sites":2750},"ex":"LDA at −78 °C removes the more accessible proton fastest and the enolates do not equilibrate: the less substituted (kinetic) enolate."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which α-carbon forms the silyl enol ether?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2 (CHCH₃)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C6 (CH₂)"}],"t":"2-Methylcyclohexanone","p":"Reagents: Et₃N, Me₃SiCl, DMF, reflux","fig":{"sites":2751},"ex":"Under equilibrating conditions the more substituted enolate, with the more substituted C=C, wins (thermodynamic control)."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which α-carbon becomes part of the enamine C=C?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C6 (CH₂)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2 (CHCH₃)"}],"t":"2-Methylcyclohexanone","p":"Reagents: pyrrolidine, TsOH (cat.), −H₂O","fig":{"sites":2750},"ex":"In the more substituted enamine the methyl would clash with the pyrrolidine ring (A¹·³ strain), so the less substituted enamine forms.","h":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2752},{"m":2753}],"t":"Cyclohexenone","fig":{"r":[2754],"cond":["(CH₃)₂CuLi, then H₃O⁺"]},"ex":"Cuprates add 1,4 (conjugate addition)."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2753},{"m":2752}],"t":"Cyclohexenone","fig":{"r":[2754],"cond":["CH₃Li, then H₃O⁺"]},"ex":"Organolithiums add fast and irreversibly to the carbonyl carbon (1,2-addition)."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2755},{"m":2756}],"t":"Cyclohexenone","fig":{"r":[2754],"cond":["NaBH₄, CeCl₃, MeOH (Luche)"]},"ex":"With CeCl₃ the borohydride attacks only the carbonyl carbon (1,2): the allylic alcohol, not the saturated ketone."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2757},{"m":2758}],"t":"Acetophenone","fig":{"r":[2759],"cond":["mCPBA (Baeyer–Villiger)"]},"ex":"The group that best carries positive charge migrates to oxygen: aryl beats methyl."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2760},{"m":2761}],"t":"Cyclohexyl methyl ketone","fig":{"r":[2762],"cond":["mCPBA (Baeyer–Villiger)"]},"ex":"A secondary alkyl group migrates before a methyl."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2763},{"m":2764}],"t":"2-Methylcyclohexanone","fig":{"r":[2765],"cond":["mCPBA (Baeyer–Villiger)"]},"ex":"Oxygen goes in on the more substituted side: the secondary carbon migrates."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which carbon does methoxide attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"CH₂ (less substituted)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"CH (more substituted)"}],"t":"Propylene oxide","p":"Reagents: NaOMe, MeOH","fig":{"sites":2766},"ex":"Under basic conditions the nucleophile attacks by SN2 at the less hindered carbon."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which carbon does methanol attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"CH (more substituted)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"CH₂ (less substituted)"}],"t":"Propylene oxide","p":"Reagents: MeOH, H₂SO₄ (cat.)","fig":{"sites":2767},"ex":"The protonated epoxide opens with a lot of SN1 character: the more substituted carbon carries more of the positive charge."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which carbon does methanol attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"benzylic CH"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"CH₂"}],"t":"Styrene oxide","p":"Reagents: MeOH, H₂SO₄ (cat.)","fig":{"sites":2768},"ex":"The benzylic carbon carries the positive charge best."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2769},{"m":2770}],"t":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane","fig":{"r":[2771],"cond":["NaOEt, EtOH, heat"]},"ex":"With a small base the more substituted alkene forms (Zaitsev)."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2770},{"m":2769}],"t":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane","fig":{"r":[2771],"cond":["KOtBu, tBuOH, heat"]},"ex":"A bulky base takes the more accessible CH₃ proton (Hofmann product)."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2772},{"m":2773}],"t":"sec-Butyltrimethylammonium hydroxide","fig":{"r":[2774],"cond":["heat (Hofmann elimination)"]},"ex":"Hofmann eliminations give the less substituted alkene: the bulky NMe₃⁺ group and an early, carbanion-like transition state favour removal of a CH₃ proton."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2728},{"m":2775}],"t":"2-Methylcyclohexanol","fig":{"r":[2776],"cond":["H₃PO₄, heat"]},"ex":"E1 through a carbocation: the most substituted alkene forms."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which OH is silylated?","o":[{"pre":"O atom","x":"primary OH"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"secondary OH"}],"t":"Butane-1,3-diol","p":"Reagents: TBSCl (1 equiv), imidazole, DMF","fig":{"sites":2777},"ex":"Silyl chlorides are sensitive to steric hindrance: the primary OH is silylated first."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which OH is tritylated?","o":[{"pre":"O atom","x":"primary OH"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"secondary OH"}],"t":"Propane-1,2-diol","p":"Reagents: TrCl, pyridine","fig":{"sites":2778},"ex":"The trityl group is so bulky that it goes almost only onto primary alcohols."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which OH is protected?","o":[{"pre":"O atom","x":"5′-OH (primary)"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"3′-OH (secondary)"}],"t":"Thymidine","p":"Reagents: DMTrCl, pyridine","fig":{"sites":2779},"ex":"The first step of DNA synthesis on a solid support: the dimethoxytrityl group protects the primary 5′-OH."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which OH is acetylated?","o":[{"pre":"O atom","x":"primary OH"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"tertiary OH"}],"t":"3-Methylbutane-1,3-diol","p":"Reagents: Ac₂O, pyridine, 0 °C","fig":{"sites":2780},"ex":"Without DMAP, tertiary alcohols are acylated very slowly."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which OH is silylated?","o":[{"pre":"O atom","x":"secondary OH"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"tertiary OH"}],"t":"2-Methylpentane-2,4-diol","p":"Reagents: TBSCl, imidazole, DMF","fig":{"sites":2781},"ex":"TBSCl does not silylate tertiary alcohols under these conditions; TBSOTf with 2,6-lutidine would."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which OH is tosylated?","o":[{"pre":"O atom","x":"primary OH"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"secondary OH"}],"t":"Pentane-1,4-diol","p":"Reagents: TsCl (1 equiv), pyridine, 0 °C","fig":{"sites":2782},"ex":"Sulfonylation is also sensitive to steric hindrance: the primary tosylate forms."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which OH is oxidised?","o":[{"pre":"O atom","x":"primary OH"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"secondary OH"}],"t":"Pentane-1,4-diol","p":"Reagents: TEMPO (cat.), NaOCl, KBr, CH₂Cl₂/H₂O, 0 °C","fig":{"sites":2782},"ex":"The bulky oxoammonium ion oxidises primary alcohols much faster than secondary ones (Anelli oxidation)."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which OH is oxidised?","o":[{"pre":"O atom","x":"allylic OH"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"non-allylic OH"}],"t":"(E)-Hex-2-ene-1,6-diol","p":"Reagents: MnO₂, CH₂Cl₂","fig":{"sites":2783},"ex":"Activated MnO₂ only oxidises allylic and benzylic alcohols."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which OH is replaced by Cl?","o":[{"pre":"O atom","x":"tertiary OH"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"primary OH"}],"t":"3-Methylbutane-1,3-diol","p":"Reagents: conc. HCl","fig":{"sites":2784},"ex":"SN1: only the tertiary alcohol forms a carbocation easily, so it becomes the tertiary chloride."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which TBS ether is cleaved first?","o":[{"pre":"O atom","x":"primary OTBS"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"secondary OTBS"}],"t":"1,3-Bis(TBS-oxy)butane","p":"Reagents: CSA (cat.), MeOH, 0 °C","fig":{"sites":2785},"ex":"Acid-catalysed desilylation is also sensitive to steric hindrance: a primary TBS ether comes off well before a secondary one."},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which acetonide is hydrolysed?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"5,6-acetonide (on the side chain)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"1,2-acetonide (fused to the ring)"}],"t":"Diacetone-D-glucose","p":"Reagents: 80% aq. AcOH, rt","fig":{"sites":2786},"ex":"The terminal 5,6-acetonide hydrolyses much faster; the 1,2-acetonide, fused to the furanose ring, survives. The product is 1,2-O-isopropylidene-α-D-glucofuranose.","h":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which oxygen keeps the benzyl group?","o":[{"pre":"O atom","x":"O6"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"O4"}],"t":"Methyl 4,6-O-benzylidene-α-D-glucopyranoside","p":"Reagents: NaCNBH₃, HCl, THF (Garegg)","fig":{"sites":2787},"ex":"Garegg's conditions give the 6-O-benzyl ether with a free 4-OH. LiAlH₄–AlCl₃ gives the reverse: 4-O-benzyl, free 6-OH.","h":true},{"s":"rl","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which oxygen keeps the benzyl group?","o":[{"pre":"O atom","x":"O4"},{"pre":"O atom","x":"O6"}],"t":"Methyl 4,6-O-benzylidene-α-D-glucopyranoside","p":"Reagents: LiAlH₄, AlCl₃","fig":{"sites":2788},"ex":"LiAlH₄–AlCl₃ gives the 4-O-benzyl ether with a free 6-OH; Garegg's NaCNBH₃–HCl gives the reverse.","h":true},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−0.81 V"},{"x":"−1.43 V","w":"Cu(dap)₂⁺ · Cu(II)/*Cu(I)"},{"x":"+0.77 V","w":"Ru(bpy)₃²⁺ · *Ru(II)/Ru(I)"},{"x":"+0.31 V","w":"fac-Ir(ppy)₃ · *Ir(III)/Ir(II)"}],"t":"Ru(bpy)₃²⁺ · Ru(III)/*Ru(II)","f":"excited state as a reductant: oxidative quenching","fig":{"m":2789},"ex":"E½ = −0.81 V vs SCE in MeCN. The benchmark. Light turns a mild ground state into both a fair reductant (−0.81 V) and a fair oxidant (+0.77 V); excited-state lifetime 1100 ns.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013, Table 1","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+0.77 V"},{"x":"−0.21 V","w":"Ru(bpm)₃²⁺ · Ru(III)/*Ru(II)"},{"x":"−2.19 V","w":"fac-Ir(ppy)₃ · Ir(III)/Ir(II)"},{"x":"+1.69 V","w":"Ir[dF(CF₃)ppy]₂(dtbbpy)⁺ · Ir(IV)/Ir(III)"}],"t":"Ru(bpy)₃²⁺ · *Ru(II)/Ru(I)","f":"excited state as an oxidant: reductive quenching","fig":{"m":2789},"ex":"E½ = +0.77 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013, Table 1","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.29 V"},{"x":"−0.96 V","w":"Ir(ppy)₂(dtbbpy)⁺ · Ir(IV)/*Ir(III)"},{"x":"+0.77 V","w":"Ru(bpy)₃²⁺ · *Ru(II)/Ru(I)"},{"x":"−1.37 V","w":"Ir[dF(CF₃)ppy]₂(dtbbpy)⁺ · Ir(III)/Ir(II)"}],"t":"Ru(bpy)₃²⁺ · Ru(III)/Ru(II)","f":"oxidised catalyst as an oxidant: closes an oxidative quenching cycle","fig":{"m":2789},"ex":"E½ = +1.29 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013, Table 1","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−1.33 V"},{"x":"−0.81 V","w":"Ru(bpy)₃²⁺ · Ru(III)/*Ru(II)"},{"x":"+1.69 V","w":"Ru(bpm)₃²⁺ · Ru(III)/Ru(II)"},{"x":"+1.21 V","w":"Ir[dF(CF₃)ppy]₂(dtbbpy)⁺ · *Ir(III)/Ir(II)"}],"t":"Ru(bpy)₃²⁺ · Ru(II)/Ru(I)","f":"reduced catalyst as a reductant: closes a reductive quenching cycle","fig":{"m":2789},"ex":"E½ = −1.33 V vs SCE in MeCN. Ru(bpy)₃⁺, made by reductive quenching with an amine, is the reductant in Stephenson's dehalogenations and Yoon's enone [2+2] cycloadditions.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013, Table 1","https: