. Three cyclometalated, electron-rich phenylpyridines: one of the strongest excited-state reductants among common catalysts. It reduces 1,4-dicyanobenzene (−1.61 V).","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013, Table 1","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+0.31 V"},{"x":"+1.45 V","w":"Ru(bpz)₃²⁺ · *Ru(II)/Ru(I)"},{"x":"−1.37 V","w":"Ir[dF(CF₃)ppy]₂(dtbbpy)⁺ · Ir(III)/Ir(II)"},{"x":"+0.77 V","w":"fac-Ir(ppy)₃ · Ir(IV)/Ir(III)"}],"t":"fac-Ir(ppy)₃ · *Ir(III)/Ir(II)","f":"excited state as an oxidant: reductive quenching","fig":{"m":2795},"ex":"E½ = +0.31 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013, Table 1","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+0.77 V"},{"x":"−1.33 V","w":"Ru(bpy)₃²⁺ · Ru(II)/Ru(I)"},{"x":"−0.80 V","w":"Ru(bpz)₃²⁺ · Ru(II)/Ru(I)"},{"x":"+1.21 V","w":"Ir(ppy)₂(dtbbpy)⁺ · Ir(IV)/Ir(III)"}],"t":"fac-Ir(ppy)₃ · Ir(IV)/Ir(III)","f":"oxidised catalyst as an oxidant: closes an oxidative quenching cycle","fig":{"m":2795},"ex":"E½ = +0.77 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013, Table 1","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−2.19 V"},{"x":"−0.80 V","w":"Ru(bpz)₃²⁺ · Ru(II)/Ru(I)"},{"x":"+0.82 V","w":"Ru(phen)₃²⁺ · *Ru(II)/Ru(I)"},{"x":"−1.33 V","w":"Ru(bpy)₃²⁺ · Ru(II)/Ru(I)"}],"t":"fac-Ir(ppy)₃ · Ir(III)/Ir(II)","f":"reduced catalyst as a reductant: closes a reductive quenching cycle","fig":{"m":2795},"ex":"E½ = −2.19 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013, Table 1","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−1.43 V"},{"x":"−0.89 V","w":"Ir[dF(CF₃)ppy]₂(dtbbpy)⁺ · Ir(IV)/*Ir(III)"},{"x":"+1.29 V","w":"Ru(bpy)₃²⁺ · Ru(III)/Ru(II)"},{"x":"+0.77 V","w":"fac-Ir(ppy)₃ · Ir(IV)/Ir(III)"}],"t":"Cu(dap)₂⁺ · Cu(II)/*Cu(I)","f":"excited state as a reductant: oxidative quenching","fig":{"m":2796},"ex":"E½ = −1.43 V vs SCE in MeCN. dap = 2,9-bis(4-anisyl)-1,10-phenanthroline. A copper photocatalyst (lifetime 270 ns) that reduces 4-nitrobenzyl bromide (−1.1 V).","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013, Table 1","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+0.62 V"},{"x":"−0.80 V","w":"Ru(bpz)₃²⁺ · Ru(II)/Ru(I)"},{"x":"−1.43 V","w":"Cu(dap)₂⁺ · Cu(II)/*Cu(I)"},{"x":"+1.69 V","w":"Ir[dF(CF₃)ppy]₂(dtbbpy)⁺ · Ir(IV)/Ir(III)"}],"t":"Cu(dap)₂⁺ · Cu(II)/Cu(I)","f":"oxidised catalyst as an oxidant: closes an oxidative quenching cycle","fig":{"m":2796},"ex":"E½ = +0.62 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013, Table 1","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−1.04 V"},{"x":"−0.57 V","w":"Mes-Acr-Me⁺ · Acr⁺/Acr•"},{"x":"+1.35 V","w":"4CzIPN · *PC/PC•⁻"},{"x":"+1.99 V","w":"9,10-Dicyanoanthracene · ¹DCA*/DCA•⁻"}],"t":"4CzIPN · PC•⁺/*PC","f":"excited state as a reductant: oxidative quenching","fig":{"m":2797},"ex":"E½ = −1.04 V vs SCE in MeCN. A carbazole–dicyanobenzene donor–acceptor dye, used in place of iridium catalysts.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Angew. Chem. Int. Ed. 2023, e202309684, citing the original measurement","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10952529/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.35 V"},{"x":"+2.06 V","w":"Mes-Acr-Me⁺ · *Acr⁺/Acr•"},{"x":"−0.32 V","w":"Methylene blue · MB⁺/MB•"},{"x":"−1.21 V","w":"4CzIPN · PC/PC•⁻"}],"t":"4CzIPN · *PC/PC•⁻","f":"excited state as an oxidant: reductive quenching","fig":{"m":2797},"ex":"E½ = +1.35 V vs SCE in MeCN. Close to Ir[dF(CF₃)ppy]₂(dtbbpy)⁺ (+1.21 V), so the two often stand in for each other.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Angew. Chem. Int. Ed. 2023, e202309684, citing the original measurement","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10952529/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−1.21 V"},{"x":"−0.28 V","w":"Triphenylpyrylium · TPT⁺/TPT•"},{"x":"+1.35 V","w":"4CzIPN · *PC/PC•⁻"},{"x":"+1.99 V","w":"9,10-Dicyanoanthracene · ¹DCA*/DCA•⁻"}],"t":"4CzIPN · PC/PC•⁻","f":"reduced catalyst as a reductant: closes a reductive quenching cycle","fig":{"m":2797},"ex":"E½ = −1.21 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Angew. Chem. Int. Ed. 2023, e202309684, citing the original measurement","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10952529/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+2.06 V"},{"x":"−0.57 V","w":"Mes-Acr-Me⁺ · Acr⁺/Acr•"},{"x":"−1.06 V","w":"Eosin Y · EY/EY•⁻"},{"x":"+0.83 V","w":"Eosin Y · ³EY*/EY•⁻"}],"t":"Mes-Acr-Me⁺ · *Acr⁺/Acr•","f":"excited state as an oxidant: reductive quenching","fig":{"m":2798},"ex":"E½ = +2.06 V vs SCE in MeCN. Fukuzumi's 9-mesityl-10-methylacridinium: one of the strongest excited-state oxidants in use. It oxidises alkenes to radical cations for anti-Markovnikov additions.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Hamilton & Nicewicz, J. Am. Chem. Soc. 2012","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3513336/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−0.57 V"},{"x":"+2.06 V","w":"Mes-Acr-Me⁺ · *Acr⁺/Acr•"},{"x":"−1.06 V","w":"Eosin Y · EY/EY•⁻"},{"x":"−2.10 V","w":"10-Phenylphenothiazine · PTH•⁺/*PTH"}],"t":"Mes-Acr-Me⁺ · Acr⁺/Acr•","f":"reduced catalyst as a reductant: closes a reductive quenching cycle","fig":{"m":2798},"ex":"E½ = −0.57 V vs SCE in MeCN. The acridine radical is only a moderate reductant, enough to turn the cycle over through a hydrogen-atom donor.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Hamilton & Nicewicz, J. Am. Chem. Soc. 2012","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3513336/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−0.59 V"},{"x":"+1.99 V","w":"9,10-Dicyanoanthracene · ¹DCA*/DCA•⁻"},{"x":"−1.21 V","w":"4CzIPN · PC/PC•⁻"},{"x":"−2.10 V","w":"10-Phenylphenothiazine · PTH•⁺/*PTH"}],"t":"tBu₂-Mes-Acr-Ph⁺ · Acr⁺/Acr•","f":"ground-state reduction of the acridinium","fig":{"m":2799},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = −0.59 V vs SCE in MeCN. Nicewicz's second-generation acridinium: tert-butyl groups block attack at C3 and C6, an N-phenyl replaces N-methyl. The ground-state potential is nearly that of Mes-Acr-Me⁺.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+0.83 V"},{"x":"−2.10 V","w":"10-Phenylphenothiazine · PTH•⁺/*PTH"},{"x":"−0.32 V","w":"Methylene blue · MB⁺/MB•"},{"x":"+1.99 V","w":"9,10-Dicyanoanthracene · ¹DCA*/DCA•⁻"}],"t":"Eosin Y · ³EY*/EY•⁻","f":"excited state as an oxidant: reductive quenching","fig":{"m":2800},"ex":"E½ = +0.83 V vs SCE in MeCN. An inexpensive xanthene dye. Its triplet excited state oxidises α-amino radicals in König's aldehyde alkylation, as Ru(bpy)₃²⁺ does.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−1.06 V"},{"x":"−0.32 V","w":"Methylene blue · MB⁺/MB•"},{"x":"+1.99 V","w":"9,10-Dicyanoanthracene · ¹DCA*/DCA•⁻"},{"x":"+0.83 V","w":"Eosin Y · ³EY*/EY•⁻"}],"t":"Eosin Y · EY/EY•⁻","f":"reduced catalyst as a reductant: closes a reductive quenching cycle","fig":{"m":2800},"ex":"E½ = −1.06 V vs SCE in MeCN. The radical anion then reduces bromomalonate.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−0.86 V"},{"x":"−0.32 V","w":"Methylene blue · MB⁺/MB•"},{"x":"+1.35 V","w":"4CzIPN · *PC/PC•⁻"},{"x":"+0.83 V","w":"Eosin Y · ³EY*/EY•⁻"}],"t":"Rose bengal · RB/RB•⁻","f":"ground-state reduction of the dye","fig":{"m":2801},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = −0.86 V vs SCE in MeCN. A tetraiodo xanthene dye, best known as a singlet-oxygen sensitiser.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−0.32 V"},{"x":"−1.06 V","w":"Eosin Y · EY/EY•⁻"},{"x":"+0.83 V","w":"Eosin Y · ³EY*/EY•⁻"},{"x":"+1.35 V","w":"4CzIPN · *PC/PC•⁻"}],"t":"Methylene blue · MB⁺/MB•","f":"ground-state reduction of the dye","fig":{"m":2802},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = −0.32 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−0.28 V"},{"x":"+1.35 V","w":"4CzIPN · *PC/PC•⁻"},{"x":"−1.04 V","w":"4CzIPN · PC•⁺/*PC"},{"x":"+0.83 V","w":"Eosin Y · ³EY*/EY•⁻"}],"t":"Triphenylpyrylium · TPT⁺/TPT•","f":"ground-state reduction of the pyrylium","fig":{"m":2803},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = −0.28 V vs SCE in MeCN. Easy to reduce in the ground state, so its excited state is a very strong oxidant.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−2.10 V"},{"x":"−1.21 V","w":"4CzIPN · PC/PC•⁻"},{"x":"−0.32 V","w":"Methylene blue · MB⁺/MB•"},{"x":"+1.99 V","w":"9,10-Dicyanoanthracene · ¹DCA*/DCA•⁻"}],"t":"10-Phenylphenothiazine · PTH•⁺/*PTH","f":"excited state as a reductant: oxidative quenching","fig":{"m":2804},"ex":"E½ = −2.10 V vs SCE in MeCN. An electron-rich organic dye whose excited state is a stronger reductant than *Ir(ppy)₃ (−1.73 V): it was chosen to reduce α-amido sulfonium salts down to −1.70 V.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["ACS Catal. 2025, citing the original measurement","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12090209/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.99 V"},{"x":"−1.04 V","w":"4CzIPN · PC•⁺/*PC"},{"x":"+1.35 V","w":"4CzIPN · *PC/PC•⁻"},{"x":"+0.83 V","w":"Eosin Y · ³EY*/EY•⁻"}],"t":"9,10-Dicyanoanthracene · ¹DCA*/DCA•⁻","f":"excited state as an oxidant: reductive quenching","fig":{"m":2805},"ex":"E½ = +1.99 V vs SCE in MeCN. Its singlet excited state is a strong oxidant; it oxidises benzylic amines with air as the terminal oxidant.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Chem. Sci. 2022, citing the original measurement","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8849051/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂?","o":[{"x":"+0.46 V"},{"x":"−0.02 V","w":"Cp*₂Fe⁺/Cp*₂Fe · decamethylferrocene"},{"x":"−0.87 V","w":"Cp₂Co⁺/Cp₂Co · cobaltocene"},{"x":"+1.00 V","w":"N-Methyl Hantzsch ester •⁺/HE"}],"t":"Fc⁺/Fc · ferrocenium","f":"ferrocenium as a one-electron oxidant","ex":"E½ = +0.46 V vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂. The source gives +0.00 V vs Fc⁺/Fc; E½(Fc⁺/Fc) = +0.46 V vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂. The reference couple itself: 0 V vs Fc⁺/Fc by definition, +0.46 V vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂ and +0.40 V in MeCN. A mild oxidant whose product, ferrocene, is easy to remove.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Connelly & Geiger, Chem. Rev. 1996, via Wikipedia: Cobaltocene","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cobaltocene"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂?","o":[{"x":"−0.02 V"},{"x":"+0.47 V","w":"CuCl₂/CuCl₂⁻"},{"x":"−1.48 V","w":"Cp*₂Co⁺/Cp*₂Co · decamethylcobaltocene"},{"x":"+1.00 V","w":"N-Methyl Hantzsch ester •⁺/HE"}],"t":"Cp*₂Fe⁺/Cp*₂Fe · decamethylferrocene","f":"decamethylferrocene as a reductant","ex":"E½ = −0.02 V vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂. The source gives −0.48 V vs Fc⁺/Fc; E½(Fc⁺/Fc) = +0.46 V vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂. Ten methyl groups make it about 0.5 V easier to oxidise than ferrocene.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Connelly & Geiger, Chem. Rev. 1996, via Wikipedia: Decamethylferrocene","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decamethylferrocene"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂?","o":[{"x":"−0.87 V"},{"x":"+0.71 V","w":"Bromide · Br•/Br⁻"},{"x":"−1.70 V","w":"Ni(II)ArX/Ni(I)Ar · (dtbbpy)Ni(Ar)Br"},{"x":"−0.26 V","w":"PIFA"}],"t":"Cp₂Co⁺/Cp₂Co · cobaltocene","f":"cobaltocene as a reductant","ex":"E½ = −0.87 V vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂. The source gives −1.33 V vs Fc⁺/Fc; E½(Fc⁺/Fc) = +0.46 V vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂. 19 valence electrons: the extra electron sits in an antibonding orbital, so cobaltocene gives it up easily. A reductant that dissolves in non-polar solvents.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Connelly & Geiger, Chem. Rev. 1996, via Wikipedia: Cobaltocene","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cobaltocene"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂?","o":[{"x":"−1.48 V"},{"x":"+1.00 V","w":"N-Methyl Hantzsch ester •⁺/HE"},{"x":"−0.02 V","w":"Cp*₂Fe⁺/Cp*₂Fe · decamethylferrocene"},{"x":"−0.87 V","w":"Cp₂Co⁺/Cp₂Co · cobaltocene"}],"t":"Cp*₂Co⁺/Cp*₂Co · decamethylcobaltocene","f":"decamethylcobaltocene as a reductant","ex":"E½ = −1.48 V vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂. The source gives −1.94 V vs Fc⁺/Fc; E½(Fc⁺/Fc) = +0.46 V vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂. About 0.6 V more reducing than cobaltocene, as with the iron pair.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Connelly & Geiger, Chem. Rev. 1996, via Wikipedia: Decamethylcobaltocene","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decamethylcobaltocene"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂?","o":[{"x":"+1.16 V"},{"x":"−0.02 V","w":"Cp*₂Fe⁺/Cp*₂Fe · decamethylferrocene"},{"x":"−1.12 V","w":"PIDA"},{"x":"+0.71 V","w":"Bromide · Br•/Br⁻"}],"t":"Magic blue · Ar₃N•⁺/Ar₃N","f":"tris(4-bromophenyl)aminium as an oxidant","fig":{"m":2806},"ex":"E½ = +1.16 V vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂. The source gives +0.70 V vs Fc⁺/Fc; E½(Fc⁺/Fc) = +0.46 V vs SCE in CH₂Cl₂. A stable aminium radical cation (as its SbCl₆⁻ salt). +0.67 V vs Fc⁺/Fc in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Connelly & Geiger, Chem. Rev. 1996, via Wikipedia: Magic blue","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tris(4-bromophenyl)ammoniumyl_hexachloroantimonate"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+0.76 V"},{"x":"−0.87 V","w":"Cp₂Co⁺/Cp₂Co · cobaltocene"},{"x":"−0.27 V","w":"N-Chlorosuccinimide"},{"x":"−1.70 V","w":"Ni(II)ArX/Ni(I)Ar · (dtbbpy)Ni(Ar)Br"}],"t":"BNAH•⁺/BNAH","f":"1-benzyl-1,4-dihydronicotinamide as a reductant","fig":{"m":2807},"ex":"E½ = +0.76 V vs SCE in MeCN. An NADH mimic. It quenches *Ru(bpy)₃²⁺ (+0.77 V) to give Ru(bpy)₃⁺, which reduces electron-poor alkenes.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.00 V"},{"x":"−1.48 V","w":"Cp*₂Co⁺/Cp*₂Co · decamethylcobaltocene"},{"x":"−0.87 V","w":"Cp₂Co⁺/Cp₂Co · cobaltocene"},{"x":"−0.26 V","w":"PIFA"}],"t":"N-Methyl Hantzsch ester •⁺/HE","f":"the dihydropyridine as a reductant","fig":{"m":2808},"ex":"E½ = +1.00 V vs SCE in MeCN. Harder to oxidise than BNAH (+0.76 V). In Kellogg's Ru(bpy)₃²⁺ reductions it does not quench the excited state; it is thought to react by giving a hydrogen atom instead.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+0.83 V"},{"x":"−1.70 V","w":"Ni(II)ArX/Ni(I)Ar · (dtbbpy)Ni(Ar)Br"},{"x":"−0.26 V","w":"PIFA"},{"x":"−1.12 V","w":"PIDA"}],"t":"Triethylamine •⁺/Et₃N","f":"the amine as a sacrificial reductant","fig":{"m":2809},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +0.83 V vs SCE in MeCN. Tertiary amines are the classic reductive quenchers. The amine radical cation loses an α-proton and becomes a strongly reducing α-amino radical (about −1.0 V).","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+0.26 V"},{"x":"−1.12 V","w":"PIDA"},{"x":"+0.83 V","w":"Triethylamine •⁺/Et₃N"},{"x":"−0.27 V","w":"N-Chlorosuccinimide"}],"t":"Iodide · I•/I⁻","f":"Bu₄N⁺I⁻ as a reductant","fig":{"m":2810},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +0.26 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+0.71 V"},{"x":"−0.27 V","w":"N-Chlorosuccinimide"},{"x":"−1.12 V","w":"PIDA"},{"x":"+1.16 V","w":"Magic blue · Ar₃N•⁺/Ar₃N"}],"t":"Bromide · Br•/Br⁻","f":"Bu₄N⁺Br⁻ as a reductant","fig":{"m":2811},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +0.71 V vs SCE in MeCN. Excited 4CzIPN (+1.35 V) oxidises bromide to the bromine atom in photocatalytic decarboxylative brominations.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.01 V"},{"x":"−1.12 V","w":"PIDA"},{"x":"−0.27 V","w":"N-Chlorosuccinimide"},{"x":"+0.26 V","w":"Iodide · I•/I⁻"}],"t":"Chloride · Cl•/Cl⁻","f":"Bu₄N⁺Cl⁻ as a reductant","fig":{"m":2812},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.01 V vs SCE in MeCN. The halides line up as I⁻ < Br⁻ < Cl⁻: the smaller the anion, the harder to oxidise.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−1.12 V"},{"x":"+0.26 V","w":"Iodide · I•/I⁻"},{"x":"−0.27 V","w":"N-Chlorosuccinimide"},{"x":"+1.01 V","w":"Chloride · Cl•/Cl⁻"}],"t":"PIDA","f":"(diacetoxyiodo)benzene as an oxidant","fig":{"m":2813},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = −1.12 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−0.26 V"},{"x":"+0.76 V","w":"BNAH•⁺/BNAH"},{"x":"−1.12 V","w":"PIDA"},{"x":"−1.70 V","w":"Ni(II)ArX/Ni(I)Ar · (dtbbpy)Ni(Ar)Br"}],"t":"PIFA","f":"[bis(trifluoroacetoxy)iodo]benzene as an oxidant","fig":{"m":2814},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = −0.26 V vs SCE in MeCN. Trifluoroacetates make the iodine(III) far easier to reduce than PIDA's acetates (−1.12 V).","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−0.27 V"},{"x":"−1.70 V","w":"Ni(II)ArX/Ni(I)Ar · (dtbbpy)Ni(Ar)Br"},{"x":"+0.76 V","w":"BNAH•⁺/BNAH"},{"x":"−1.12 V","w":"PIDA"}],"t":"N-Chlorosuccinimide","f":"NCS as an oxidant","fig":{"m":2815},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = −0.27 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in DMF?","o":[{"x":"−1.20 V"},{"x":"−0.27 V","w":"N-Chlorosuccinimide"},{"x":"−1.70 V","w":"Ni(II)ArX/Ni(I)Ar · (dtbbpy)Ni(Ar)Br"},{"x":"+1.00 V","w":"N-Methyl Hantzsch ester •⁺/HE"}],"t":"Ni(II)/Ni(0) · NiX₂(dtbbpy)","f":"nickel(II) reduced to nickel(0) (two electrons)","fig":{"m":2816},"ex":"E½ = −1.20 V vs SCE in DMF. A two-electron potential. In metallaphotoredox, Ir(II) (−1.37 V) is taken to reduce Ni(I) to Ni(0) on the strength of it.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Zuo et al., Science 2014 (decarboxylative arylation)","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4296524/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−1.70 V"},{"x":"−0.87 V","w":"Cp₂Co⁺/Cp₂Co · cobaltocene"},{"x":"−0.27 V","w":"N-Chlorosuccinimide"},{"x":"+0.46 V","w":"Fc⁺/Fc · ferrocenium"}],"t":"Ni(II)ArX/Ni(I)Ar · (dtbbpy)Ni(Ar)Br","f":"the oxidative-addition complex reduced to Ni(I)","fig":{"m":2817},"ex":"E½ = −1.70 V vs SCE in MeCN. Too negative for Ir(II) (−1.37 V), so MacMillan ruled out reduction of the Ni(II) aryl complex.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Zuo et al., Science 2014 (decarboxylative arylation)","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4296524/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+0.47 V"},{"x":"+1.00 V","w":"N-Methyl Hantzsch ester •⁺/HE"},{"x":"−1.12 V","w":"PIDA"},{"x":"−1.70 V","w":"Ni(II)ArX/Ni(I)Ar · (dtbbpy)Ni(Ar)Br"}],"t":"CuCl₂/CuCl₂⁻","f":"copper(II) chloride as an oxidant","fig":{"m":2818},"ex":"E½ = +0.47 V vs SCE in MeCN. Copper(II) takes an electron easily; reduced Ir(II) or excited catalysts turn it into Cu(I), the species that captures radicals in photoredox–copper dual catalysis.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Angew. Chem. Int. Ed. 2023, e202309684, citing the original measurement","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10952529/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+2.36 V"},{"x":"+1.50 V","w":"sec-Alkyl trifluoroborate (K⁺ salt)"},{"x":"+0.74 V","w":"N,N-Dimethylaniline"},{"x":"+1.94 V","w":"Furan"}],"t":"Toluene","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2819},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +2.36 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+2.07 V"},{"x":"+1.56 V","w":"Diphenyl disulfide"},{"x":"+1.10 V","w":"Benzyl trifluoroborate (K⁺ salt)"},{"x":"+0.54 V","w":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene"}],"t":"Mesitylene","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2820},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +2.07 V vs SCE in MeCN. Close to the true E½ (2.05 V); the peak potential is 2.3 V. The half-peak potential is the better estimate for irreversible waves.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.64 V"},{"x":"+0.54 V","w":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene"},{"x":"+2.26 V","w":"N,N-Dimethylformamide"},{"x":"+1.17 V","w":"4-Methoxyphenol"}],"t":"Naphthalene","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2821},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.64 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.81 V"},{"x":"+1.17 V","w":"4-Methoxyphenol"},{"x":"+0.74 V","w":"N,N-Dimethylaniline"},{"x":"+2.37 V","w":"Cyclohexene"}],"t":"Anisole","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2822},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.81 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.30 V"},{"x":"+1.94 V","w":"Furan"},{"x":"+0.54 V","w":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene"},{"x":"+2.36 V","w":"Toluene"}],"t":"1,4-Dimethoxybenzene","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2823},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.30 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.97 V"},{"x":"+1.24 V","w":"trans-Anethole"},{"x":"+0.74 V","w":"N,N-Dimethylaniline"},{"x":"+2.37 V","w":"Cyclohexene"}],"t":"Styrene","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2724},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.97 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.24 V"},{"x":"+1.74 V","w":"trans-β-Methylstyrene"},{"x":"+2.36 V","w":"Toluene"},{"x":"+0.74 V","w":"N,N-Dimethylaniline"}],"t":"trans-Anethole","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2824},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.24 V vs SCE in MeCN. The para-methoxy group lowers the potential by 0.5 V compared with β-methylstyrene (+1.74 V).","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.74 V"},{"x":"+1.30 V","w":"1,4-Dimethoxybenzene"},{"x":"+2.19 V","w":"Benzyl alcohol"},{"x":"+0.74 V","w":"N,N-Dimethylaniline"}],"t":"trans-β-Methylstyrene","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2825},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.74 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+2.37 V"},{"x":"+1.81 V","w":"Anisole"},{"x":"+1.16 V","w":"Indole"},{"x":"+0.54 V","w":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene"}],"t":"Cyclohexene","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2826},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +2.37 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.98 V"},{"x":"+1.50 V","w":"sec-Alkyl trifluoroborate (K⁺ salt)"},{"x":"+1.04 V","w":"N-Methylpyrrole"},{"x":"+0.54 V","w":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene"}],"t":"2-Methyl-2-butene","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2769},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.98 V vs SCE in MeCN. Each alkyl group on the alkene makes it easier to oxidise.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.63 V"},{"x":"+2.26 V","w":"N,N-Dimethylformamide"},{"x":"+1.04 V","w":"N-Methylpyrrole"},{"x":"+0.54 V","w":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene"}],"t":"Phenol","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2827},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.63 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.17 V"},{"x":"+1.74 V","w":"trans-β-Methylstyrene"},{"x":"+2.26 V","w":"N,N-Dimethylformamide"},{"x":"+0.54 V","w":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene"}],"t":"4-Methoxyphenol","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2828},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.17 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.44 V"},{"x":"+2.36 V","w":"Toluene"},{"x":"+0.95 V","w":"Boc-Pro-OCs"},{"x":"+1.84 V","w":"Diphenylacetylene"}],"t":"Thioanisole","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2829},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.44 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.56 V"},{"x":"+2.36 V","w":"Toluene"},{"x":"+0.74 V","w":"N,N-Dimethylaniline"},{"x":"+1.16 V","w":"Indole"}],"t":"Diphenyl disulfide","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2830},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.56 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.94 V"},{"x":"+1.52 V","w":"4-Methoxybenzyl alcohol"},{"x":"+0.54 V","w":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene"},{"x":"+2.37 V","w":"Cyclohexene"}],"t":"Furan","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2831},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.94 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.16 V"},{"x":"+1.56 V","w":"Diphenyl disulfide"},{"x":"+2.37 V","w":"Cyclohexene"},{"x":"+1.97 V","w":"Styrene"}],"t":"Indole","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2832},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.16 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.04 V"},{"x":"+1.50 V","w":"sec-Alkyl trifluoroborate (K⁺ salt)"},{"x":"+1.97 V","w":"Styrene"},{"x":"+2.37 V","w":"Cyclohexene"}],"t":"N-Methylpyrrole","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2833},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.04 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+0.74 V"},{"x":"+1.64 V","w":"Naphthalene"},{"x":"+2.19 V","w":"Benzyl alcohol"},{"x":"+1.16 V","w":"Indole"}],"t":"N,N-Dimethylaniline","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2834},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +0.74 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+0.89 V"},{"x":"+1.84 V","w":"Diphenylacetylene"},{"x":"+2.26 V","w":"N,N-Dimethylformamide"},{"x":"+1.44 V","w":"Thioanisole"}],"t":"Pyrrolidine","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2835},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +0.89 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+0.54 V"},{"x":"+1.64 V","w":"Naphthalene"},{"x":"+2.36 V","w":"Toluene"},{"x":"+1.04 V","w":"N-Methylpyrrole"}],"t":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2836},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +0.54 V vs SCE in MeCN. An enamine: the nitrogen lone pair is conjugated with the alkene, so it is oxidised more easily than either an amine or an alkene.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+2.26 V"},{"x":"+1.52 V","w":"4-Methoxybenzyl alcohol"},{"x":"+0.95 V","w":"Boc-Pro-OCs"},{"x":"+0.54 V","w":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene"}],"t":"N,N-Dimethylformamide","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2837},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +2.26 V vs SCE in MeCN. Amides are far harder to oxidise than amines (+0.74 V for N,N-dimethylaniline): no common photocatalyst oxidises them.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.84 V"},{"x":"+0.74 V","w":"N,N-Dimethylaniline"},{"x":"+1.24 V","w":"trans-Anethole"},{"x":"+2.37 V","w":"Cyclohexene"}],"t":"Diphenylacetylene","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2838},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = +1.84 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+0.95 V"},{"x":"+1.64 V","w":"Naphthalene"},{"x":"+2.36 V","w":"Toluene"},{"x":"+0.54 V","w":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene"}],"t":"Boc-Pro-OCs","f":"oxidation of the carboxylate","fig":{"m":2839},"ex":"E½ = +0.95 V vs SCE in MeCN. An amino-acid carboxylate: after oxidation it loses CO₂ to give an α-amino radical (MacMillan's decarboxylative arylation).","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Zuo et al., Science 2014 (decarboxylative arylation)","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4296524/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.10 V"},{"x":"+1.98 V","w":"2-Methyl-2-butene"},{"x":"+1.56 V","w":"Diphenyl disulfide"},{"x":"+0.54 V","w":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene"}],"t":"Benzyl trifluoroborate (K⁺ salt)","f":"oxidation of the borate","fig":{"m":2840},"ex":"E½ = +1.10 V vs SCE in MeCN. Oxidation breaks the C–B bond and releases a benzyl radical: Molander's single-electron transmetalation.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Primer, Karakaya, Tellis & Molander, J. Am. Chem. Soc. 2015","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4466116/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.50 V"},{"x":"+2.19 V","w":"Benzyl alcohol"},{"x":"+0.54 V","w":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene"},{"x":"+0.95 V","w":"Boc-Pro-OCs"}],"t":"sec-Alkyl trifluoroborate (K⁺ salt)","f":"oxidation of the borate","fig":{"m":2841},"ex":"E½ = +1.50 V vs SCE in MeCN. 0.4 V harder to oxidise than benzylic borates, beyond the reach of Ir[dF(CF₃)ppy]₂(bpy)⁺ (+1.32 V); Molander found they still couple.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Primer, Karakaya, Tellis & Molander, J. Am. Chem. Soc. 2015","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4466116/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+1.52 V"},{"x":"+2.36 V","w":"Toluene"},{"x":"+0.54 V","w":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene"},{"x":"+1.04 V","w":"N-Methylpyrrole"}],"t":"4-Methoxybenzyl alcohol","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2842},"ex":"E½ = +1.52 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E½ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"+2.19 V"},{"x":"+0.54 V","w":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene"},{"x":"+1.24 V","w":"trans-Anethole"},{"x":"+1.74 V","w":"trans-β-Methylstyrene"}],"t":"Benzyl alcohol","f":"oxidation","fig":{"m":2843},"ex":"E½ = +2.19 V vs SCE in MeCN. 0.7 V harder to oxidise than 4-methoxybenzyl alcohol (+1.52 V).","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Easiest to oxidise","a":"Which of these is the easiest to oxidise?","o":[{"m":2839,"x":"Boc-Pro-OCs","v":"+0.95 V"},{"m":2842,"x":"4-Methoxybenzyl alcohol","v":"+1.52 V"},{"m":2724,"x":"Styrene","v":"+1.97 V"},{"m":2826,"x":"Cyclohexene","v":"+2.37 V"}],"ex":"E½ vs SCE in MeCN: Boc-Pro-OCs +0.95 V < 4-Methoxybenzyl alcohol +1.52 V < Styrene +1.97 V < Cyclohexene +2.37 V. The least positive E(S•⁺/S) gives up its electron most easily.","lk":[{"h":"Sources","l":[["Boc-Pro-OCs","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4296524/"],["4-Methoxybenzyl alcohol","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"],["Styrene","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"],["Cyclohexene","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Easiest to oxidise","a":"Which of these is the easiest to oxidise?","o":[{"m":2839,"x":"Boc-Pro-OCs","v":"+0.95 V"},{"m":2844,"x":"Acetate (Bu₄N⁺ salt)","v":"+1.47 V"},{"m":2831,"x":"Furan","v":"+1.94 V"},{"m":2819,"x":"Toluene","v":"+2.36 V"}],"ex":"E½ vs SCE in MeCN: Boc-Pro-OCs +0.95 V < Acetate (Bu₄N⁺ salt) +1.47 V < Furan +1.94 V < Toluene +2.36 V. The least positive E(S•⁺/S) gives up its electron most easily.","lk":[{"h":"Sources","l":[["Boc-Pro-OCs","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4296524/"],["Acetate (Bu₄N⁺ salt)","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"],["Furan","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"],["Toluene","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Easiest to oxidise","a":"Which of these is the easiest to oxidise?","o":[{"m":2836,"x":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene","v":"+0.54 V"},{"m":2839,"x":"Boc-Pro-OCs","v":"+0.95 V"},{"m":2829,"x":"Thioanisole","v":"+1.44 V"},{"m":2820,"x":"Mesitylene","v":"+2.07 V"}],"ex":"Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN: 1-Morpholinocyclohexene +0.54 V < Boc-Pro-OCs +0.95 V < Thioanisole +1.44 V < Mesitylene +2.07 V. The least positive E(S•⁺/S) gives up its electron most easily.","lk":[{"h":"Sources","l":[["1-Morpholinocyclohexene","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"],["Boc-Pro-OCs","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4296524/"],["Thioanisole","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"],["Mesitylene","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Easiest to oxidise","a":"Which of these is the easiest to oxidise?","o":[{"m":2836,"x":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene","v":"+0.54 V"},{"m":2832,"x":"Indole","v":"+1.16 V"},{"m":2830,"x":"Diphenyl disulfide","v":"+1.56 V"},{"m":2843,"x":"Benzyl alcohol","v":"+2.19 V"}],"ex":"Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN: 1-Morpholinocyclohexene +0.54 V < Indole +1.16 V < Diphenyl disulfide +1.56 V < Benzyl alcohol +2.19 V. The least positive E(S•⁺/S) gives up its electron most easily.","lk":[{"h":"Sources","l":[["1-Morpholinocyclohexene","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"],["Indole","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"],["Diphenyl disulfide","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"],["Benzyl alcohol","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Easiest to oxidise","a":"Which of these is the easiest to oxidise?","o":[{"m":2836,"x":"1-Morpholinocyclohexene","v":"+0.54 V"},{"m":2840,"x":"Benzyl trifluoroborate (K⁺ salt)","v":"+1.10 V"},{"m":2831,"x":"Furan","v":"+1.94 V"},{"m":2819,"x":"Toluene","v":"+2.36 V"}],"ex":"Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN: 1-Morpholinocyclohexene +0.54 V < Benzyl trifluoroborate (K⁺ salt) +1.10 V < Furan +1.94 V < Toluene +2.36 V. The least positive E(S•⁺/S) gives up its electron most easily.","lk":[{"h":"Sources","l":[["1-Morpholinocyclohexene","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"],["Benzyl trifluoroborate (K⁺ salt)","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4466116/"],["Furan","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"],["Toluene","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−0.62 V"},{"x":"−1.89 V","w":"4'-Bromoacetophenone"},{"x":"−0.20 V","w":"Trifluoroacetic anhydride"},{"x":"−1.22 V","w":"CF₃I"}],"t":"Diethyl bromomalonate","f":"reduction","fig":{"m":2845},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = −0.62 V vs SCE in MeCN. Two esters make the C–Br bond easy to reduce: the radical anion falls apart into a malonyl radical and Br⁻.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−1.08 V"},{"x":"−0.45 V","w":"Benzenesulfonyl chloride"},{"x":"−1.95 V","w":"1-Bromo-4-iodobenzene"},{"x":"−2.43 V","w":"Benzonitrile"}],"t":"Ethyl bromoacetate","f":"reduction","fig":{"m":2846},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = −1.08 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its Eₚ/₂ vs SCE in MeCN?","o":[{"x":"−1.82 V"},{"x":"−0.93 V","w":"4'-Nitroacetophenone"},{"x":"−0.10 V","w":"Benzenediazonium"},{"x":"−1.42 V","w":"4-Cyanobenzaldehyde"}],"t":"Diiodomethane","f":"reduction","fig":{"m":2847},"ex":"Eₚ/₂ = −1.82 V vs SCE in MeCN.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz