x":"E½ vs SCE in MeCN: Bromotrichloromethane −0.18 V > Ethyl bromoacetate −1.08 V > Diiodomethane −1.82 V > Cyclohexanone −2.33 V. The least negative E(S/S•⁻) takes an electron most easily.","lk":[{"h":"Sources","l":[["Bromotrichloromethane","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"],["Ethyl bromoacetate","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"],["Diiodomethane","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"],["Cyclohexanone","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E° vs SHE in water?","o":[{"x":"−3.04 V"},{"x":"−2.37 V","w":"Mg²⁺/Mg"},{"x":"+1.33 V","w":"Cr₂O₇²⁻/Cr³⁺"},{"x":"+0.80 V","w":"Ag⁺/Ag"}],"t":"Li⁺/Li","f":"lithium metal as a reductant","fig":{"m":2870},"ex":"E° = −3.04 V vs SHE in water. The most negative standard potential: the small Li⁺ ion is very strongly hydrated.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Standard electrode potential (data page), Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_electrode_potential_(data_page)"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E° vs SHE in water?","o":[{"x":"−2.93 V"},{"x":"+1.36 V","w":"Cl₂/Cl⁻"},{"x":"+0.16 V","w":"Cu²⁺/Cu⁺"},{"x":"+1.92 V","w":"Co³⁺/Co²⁺"}],"t":"K⁺/K","f":"potassium metal as a reductant","fig":{"m":2871},"ex":"E° = −2.93 V vs SHE in water.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Standard electrode potential (data page), Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_electrode_potential_(data_page)"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E° vs SHE in water?","o":[{"x":"−2.71 V"},{"x":"+1.61 V","w":"Ce⁴⁺/Ce³⁺"},{"x":"−0.41 V","w":"Cr³⁺/Cr²⁺"},{"x":"+0.00 V","w":"2H⁺/H₂"}],"t":"Na⁺/Na","f":"sodium metal as a reductant","fig":{"m":2872},"ex":"E° = −2.71 V vs SHE in water.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Standard electrode potential (data page), Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_electrode_potential_(data_page)"]]}]},{"s":"pr","k":"Potential","a":"What is its E° vs SHE in water?","o":[{"x":"−2.37 V"},{"x":"+1.78 V","w":"H₂O₂/H₂O"},{"x