1 V).","h":true,"lk":[{"h":"Diphenylacetylene","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]},{"h":"Mes-Acr-Me⁺ · *Acr⁺/Acr•","l":[["Hamilton & Nicewicz, J. Am. Chem. Soc. 2012","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3513336/"]]}],"x":true},{"s":"pr","k":"Electron transfer","a":"Can the donor give one electron to the acceptor, downhill?","o":[{"x":"Yes, downhill"},{"x":"No, uphill"}],"y":1,"fig":{"pair":[2809,2854],"names":["Triethylamine •⁺/Et₃N","1,4-Dicyanobenzene"],"subs":["the amine as a sacrificial reductant","reduction"],"roles":["Donor","Acceptor"]},"ex":"ΔG ≈ E(donor) − E(acceptor) = +0.83 − (−1.64) = +2.47 eV: uphill, the electron stays.","lk":[{"h":"Triethylamine •⁺/Et₃N","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]},{"h":"1,4-Dicyanobenzene","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}],"x":true},{"s":"pr","k":"Electron transfer","a":"Can the donor give one electron to the acceptor, downhill?","o":[{"x":"Yes, downhill"},{"x":"No, uphill"}],"fig":{"pair":[2790,2860],"names":["Ru(bpz)₃²⁺ · Ru(II)/Ru(I)","Trifluoroacetic anhydride"],"subs":["reduced catalyst as a reductant: closes a reductive quenching cycle","reduction"],"roles":["Donor","Acceptor"]},"ex":"ΔG ≈ E(donor) − E(acceptor) = −0.80 − (−0.20) = −0.60 eV: downhill, the electron can move.","lk":[{"h":"Ru(bpz)₃²⁺ · Ru(II)/Ru(I)","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013, Table 1","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]},{"h":"Trifluoroacetic anhydride","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}],"x":true},{"s":"pr","k":"Electron transfer","a":"Can the donor give one electron to the acceptor, downhill?","o":[{"x":"Yes, downhill"},{"x":"No, uphill"}],"y":1,"fig":{"pair":[2794,2850],"names":["Ir(ppy)₂(dtbbpy)⁺ · Ir(IV)/*Ir(III)","1-Bromo-4-iodobenzene"],"subs":["excited state as a reductant: oxidative quenching","reduction"],"roles":["Donor","Acceptor"]},"ex":"ΔG ≈ E(donor) − E(acceptor) = −0.96 − (−1.95) = +0.99 eV: uphill, the electron stays.","lk":[{"h":"Ir(ppy)₂(dtbbpy)⁺ · Ir(IV)/*Ir(III)","l":[["Prier, Rankic & MacMillan, Chem. Rev. 2013, Table 1","https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4028850/"]]},{"h":"1-Bromo-4-iodobenzene","l":[["Roth, Romero & Nicewicz, Synlett 2016","https://doi.org/10.1055/s-0035-1561297"]]}],"x":true},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"},{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"}],"t":"tert-Butyl radical","fig":{"m":2929},"ex":"Three alkyl groups donate into the singly occupied orbital (SOMO) by hyperconjugation and raise it: tertiary alkyl radicals are strongly nucleophilic and add fast to acrylates (Giese reaction). The bulk does not slow them as it would an anion, because the transition state is early, with a long forming bond (2.55 Å for t-Bu• adding to methyl vinyl ketone).","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"},{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"}],"t":"Isopropyl radical","fig":{"m":2930},"ex":"Secondary alkyl radicals are nucleophilic: two alkyl groups raise the SOMO. Ethyl and isopropyl radicals made from ethane and propane add to Michael acceptors.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"},{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"}],"t":"Methyl radical","fig":{"m":2931},"ex":"Ambiphilic: the high-energy methyl radical barely discriminates between electron-rich and electron-poor alkenes. Under mild conditions it reacts as a nucleophile, adding to Michael acceptors and to azodicarboxylates.","h":true,"lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"Cyanomethyl radical","fig":{"m":2932},"ex":"One electron-withdrawing group is enough to make a primary radical electrophilic: the nitrile's π* accepts density from the SOMO and lowers it. Cyanoalkyl radicals add to electron-rich enamines.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"(Ethoxycarbonyl)methyl radical","fig":{"m":2933},"ex":"A primary radical with one ester is electrophilic: α-carbonyl radicals add to enamines and enol ethers.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"},{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"}],"t":"2-(Methoxycarbonyl)prop-2-yl radical","fig":{"m":2934},"ex":"A tertiary radical with a single ester is still nucleophilic: it is the growing chain end when methyl methacrylate polymerises, adding to the electron-poor monomer. Secondary and tertiary radicals need two electron-withdrawing groups to turn electrophilic.","h":true,"lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"Diethyl malonyl radical","fig":{"m":2935},"ex":"Two esters lower the SOMO enough to make even a secondary radical electrophilic: malonyl radicals add to indoles and other electron-rich arenes.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"},{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"}],"t":"Hydroxymethyl radical","fig":{"m":2936},"ex":"The oxygen lone pair donates into the SOMO (n → p) and raises it: α-oxy radicals are strongly nucleophilic, even though oxygen is electronegative.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"},{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"}],"t":"Tetrahydrofuran-2-yl radical","fig":{"m":2937},"ex":"An α-oxy radical, among the most nucleophilic radicals known. Cl• or decatungstate hydrogen-atom transfer makes it from THF, and it adds to electron-poor alkenes or to nickel.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"},{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"}],"t":"N-Boc-pyrrolidin-2-yl radical","fig":{"m":2938},"ex":"Nitrogen is less electronegative than oxygen, so α-amino radicals are even more nucleophilic, and they stay so with a Boc group on nitrogen. They add to protonated heteroarenes (Minisci reactions) and are captured by nickel.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"},{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"}],"t":"(Methylthio)methyl radical","fig":{"m":2939},"ex":"α-Sulfur radicals, like α-F, α-Cl, α-Br and α-I radicals, are moderately nucleophilic: the lone pair donates by π overlap. Made by metal-hydride HAT from vinyl sulfides, they add to acrylamides.","h":true,"lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"(Trimethylsilyl)methyl radical","fig":{"m":2940},"ex":"A primary α-silyl radical is electrophilic: the SOMO gives density to silicon's low-lying acceptor orbitals, which lowers it. With two more alkyl groups (a tertiary α-silyl radical) it is nucleophilic again.","h":true,"lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"},{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"}],"t":"2-(Dimethylphenylsilyl)prop-2-yl radical","fig":{"m":2941},"ex":"A tertiary α-silyl radical is nucleophilic: it adds to methyl acrylate (Giese). The two alkyl groups outweigh silicon's acceptor effect, which wins only in primary and secondary α-silyl radicals.","h":true,"lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"Trifluoromethyl radical","fig":{"m":2942},"ex":"Mostly electrophilic: three electronegative fluorines outweigh their π donation, and •CF₃ adds to electron-rich arenes and alkenes. It is ambiphilic, though, and also adds to some electron-poor heteroarenes and alkenes.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"},{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"}],"t":"Difluoromethyl radical","fig":{"m":2943},"ex":"Unlike •CF₃, •CF₂H reacts as a nucleophile with heteroarenes: it attacks protonated, electron-poor positions as alkyl radicals do in Minisci reactions.","h":true,"lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Nat. Commun. 2024: why •CF₂H is nucleophilic","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-48949-z"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"Phenyl radical","fig":{"m":2944},"ex":"A σ radical: the SOMO is an sp² orbital in the ring plane, at right angles to the π system, so ring substituents barely change it. Its s character makes it electrophilic, and its high energy makes it ambiphilic: aryl radicals add to electron-rich and electron-poor arenes alike.","h":true,"lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"2-Methylprop-1-enyl radical","fig":{"m":2945},"ex":"Vinyl radicals are σ radicals with more s character than alkyl radicals: they are electrophilic and usually add to electron-rich or plain alkenes, which is why vinyl radical cyclisations onto unactivated alkenes work.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"Phenylethynyl radical","fig":{"m":2946},"ex":"Alkynyl radicals have the most s character of all and are electrophilic: in a competition, phenylethynyl radicals attack the more electron-rich of two arenes.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"},{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"}],"t":"Acetyl radical","fig":{"m":2947},"ex":"The oxygen lone pair is held in line with the SOMO, so acyl radicals are exceptionally nucleophilic: an umpolung of the electrophilic carbonyl carbon. They add to acrylonitriles and vinyl sulfones, giving ketones.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"tert-Butoxyl radical","fig":{"m":2948},"ex":"Oxygen is far more electronegative than carbon (3.44 against 2.55), so oxyl radicals are strongly electrophilic. They seldom add to π bonds; they abstract hydridic H atoms (HAT) or fragment (β-scission).","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"Piperidin-1-yl (aminyl) radical","fig":{"m":2949},"ex":"N is more electronegative than C (3.04): aminyl radicals are electrophilic and aminate electron-rich arenes. Protonated, as aminium radical cations, they are more electrophilic still.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"Piperidinium radical cation","fig":{"m":2950},"ex":"Aminium radical cations are among the most electrophilic radicals: they add to electron-rich arenes and abstract hydridic C–H bonds next to O and N.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"N-Methylacetamidyl radical","fig":{"m":2951},"ex":"Amidyl radicals are electrophilic N radicals: they add to electron-rich π systems and abstract δ-C–H bonds (1,5-HAT), as in Hofmann–Löffler-type remote functionalisation.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"Butanethiyl radical","fig":{"m":2952},"ex":"Sulfur is barely more electronegative than carbon (2.58 against 2.55), yet thiyl radicals are electrophilic: the thiol–ene reaction is fastest with electron-rich alkenes such as vinyl ethers and norbornene.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"Bromine atom","fig":{"m":2953},"ex":"Halogen atoms are strongly electrophilic: Br• adds to electron-rich alkenes (radical HBr addition) and picks out hydridic C–H bonds.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"Chlorine atom","fig":{"m":2954},"ex":"Cl• is one of the most electrophilic radicals: it abstracts the hydridic C–H next to the oxygen of THF, and nickel then couples the α-oxy radical.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"},{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"}],"t":"Tris(trimethylsilyl)silyl radical","fig":{"m":2955},"ex":"Silicon is less electronegative than carbon, so silyl radicals are nucleophilic: (TMS)₃Si• adds to methyl acrylate but not to 1-hexene or styrene, and abstracts halogen atoms fast.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"},{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"}],"t":"NHC–boryl radical","fig":{"m":2956},"ex":"Boron is less electronegative than carbon (2.04): ligated boryl radicals are nucleophilic. An NHC–BH₂• radical added to methyl acrylate at least 380 times faster than to ethyl vinyl ether.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Radical philicity","a":"How does this radical usually react?","o":[{"x":"Electrophilic: prefers electron-rich partners"},{"x":"Nucleophilic: prefers electron-poor partners"}],"t":"Diethoxyphosphoryl radical","fig":{"m":2957},"ex":"Phosphonyl radicals, which carry a P=O, are electrophilic: they add to unactivated alkenes and enol ethers, although phosphorus is less electronegative than carbon.","h":true,"lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Polarity matching","a":"Which partner does the NHC–boryl radical react with faster?","o":[{"m":2958,"x":"methyl acrylate"},{"m":2959,"x":"ethyl vinyl ether"}],"t":"NHC–boryl radical","fig":{"m":2956},"ex":"At least 380 times faster to the acrylate: a nucleophilic radical wants an electron-poor partner.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Polarity matching","a":"Which partner does the tert-butyl radical react with faster?","o":[{"m":2960,"x":"acrylonitrile"},{"m":2959,"x":"ethyl vinyl ether"}],"t":"tert-Butyl radical","fig":{"m":2929},"ex":"The nucleophilic t-Bu• radical adds to the electron-poor alkene (a Giese addition); the electron-rich enol ether is a poor match.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Polarity matching","a":"Which partner does the diethyl malonyl radical react with faster?","o":[{"m":2959,"x":"ethyl vinyl ether"},{"m":2958,"x":"methyl acrylate"}],"t":"Diethyl malonyl radical","fig":{"m":2935},"ex":"Two esters make the malonyl radical electrophilic, so it adds to the electron-rich enol ether.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Polarity matching","a":"Which partner does the tetrahydrofuran-2-yl radical react with faster?","o":[{"m":2958,"x":"methyl acrylate"},{"m":2731,"x":"1-hexene"}],"t":"Tetrahydrofuran-2-yl radical","fig":{"m":2937},"ex":"The α-oxy radical is strongly nucleophilic and adds to the acrylate; 1-hexene offers no polar help.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Polarity matching","a":"Which partner does the butanethiyl radical react with faster?","o":[{"m":2959,"x":"ethyl vinyl ether"},{"m":2958,"x":"methyl acrylate"}],"t":"Butanethiyl radical","fig":{"m":2952},"ex":"Thiyl radicals are electrophilic: in thiol–ene chemistry vinyl ethers react far faster than acrylates, which tend to polymerise instead.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Polarity matching","a":"Which partner does the trifluoromethyl radical react with faster?","o":[{"m":2833,"x":"N-methylpyrrole"},{"m":2961,"x":"pyridine"}],"t":"Trifluoromethyl radical","fig":{"m":2942},"ex":"•CF₃ is electrophilic and attacks the electron-rich pyrrole, at C2, like an electrophile would.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Polarity matching","a":"Which partner does the acetyl radical react with faster?","o":[{"m":2960,"x":"acrylonitrile"},{"m":2959,"x":"ethyl vinyl ether"}],"t":"Acetyl radical","fig":{"m":2947},"ex":"Acyl radicals are nucleophilic and add to electron-poor alkenes, giving ketones.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Polarity matching","a":"Which partner does the tris(trimethylsilyl)silyl radical react with faster?","o":[{"m":2958,"x":"methyl acrylate"},{"m":2731,"x":"1-hexene"}],"t":"Tris(trimethylsilyl)silyl radical","fig":{"m":2955},"ex":"The nucleophilic silyl radical adds to methyl acrylate; with 1-hexene or styrene no reaction was seen.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Polarity matching","a":"Which partner does the piperidinium radical cation react with faster?","o":[{"m":2822,"x":"anisole"},{"m":2758,"x":"methyl benzoate"}],"t":"Piperidinium radical cation","fig":{"m":2950},"ex":"The electrophilic aminium radical cation aminates electron-rich arenes such as anisole.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Polarity matching","a":"Which partner does the cyanomethyl radical react with faster?","o":[{"m":2959,"x":"ethyl vinyl ether"},{"m":2960,"x":"acrylonitrile"}],"t":"Cyanomethyl radical","fig":{"m":2932},"ex":"One nitrile makes the primary radical electrophilic, so it prefers the electron-rich enol ether.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Polarity matching","a":"Which partner does the bromine atom react with faster?","o":[{"m":2731,"x":"1-hexene"},{"m":2960,"x":"acrylonitrile"}],"t":"Bromine atom","fig":{"m":2953},"ex":"Br• is electrophilic and adds to the electron-richer alkene.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Polarity matching","a":"Which partner does the N-Boc-pyrrolidin-2-yl radical react with faster?","o":[{"m":2962,"x":"4-methylquinolinium (in acid)"},{"m":2822,"x":"anisole"}],"t":"N-Boc-pyrrolidin-2-yl radical","fig":{"m":2938},"ex":"The nucleophilic α-amino radical adds to the protonated, electron-poor heteroarene: a Minisci reaction.","lk":[{"h":"Source","l":[["Parsaee et al., Nat. Rev. Chem. 2021","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41570-021-00284-3"]]}]},{"s":"rad","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which product forms?","o":[{"m":2720},{"m":2719}],"t":"Propene","fig":{"r":[2721],"cond":["HBr, peroxides"]},"ex":"A radical chain: Br· adds first, to CH₂, giving the more stable secondary radical; the H comes last."},{"s":"rad","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which C–H is brominated?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"tertiary C–H"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"secondary C–H"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"primary C–H"}],"t":"2-Methylbutane","p":"Reagents: Br₂, hν","fig":{"sites":2963},"ex":"Bromination is very selective: the tertiary radical forms fastest."},{"s":"rad","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which C–H is brominated?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"benzylic CH₂"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"middle CH₂"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"CH₃"}],"t":"Propylbenzene","p":"Reagents: NBS, (PhCO₂)₂, CCl₄","fig":{"sites":2964},"ex":"The benzylic radical is stabilised by the ring."},{"s":"rad","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does the t-Bu radical attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C3"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C8"}],"t":"4-Methylquinoline (lepidine)","p":"Reagents: t-BuCO₂H, AgNO₃, (NH₄)₂S₂O₈, H₂SO₄","fig":{"sites":2965},"ex":"A Minisci reaction: acid protonates the quinoline, which leaves C2 and C4 electron-poor, and the nucleophilic t-Bu radical (from pivalic acid) attacks there. C4 already carries the methyl."},{"s":"rad","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does the t-Bu radical attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C4"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C3"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C5"}],"t":"2-Methylquinoline (quinaldine)","p":"Reagents: t-BuCO₂H, AgNO₃, (NH₄)₂S₂O₈, H₂SO₄","fig":{"sites":2966},"ex":"With C2 blocked, the nucleophilic radical takes the other electron-poor position of the quinolinium ion, C4."},{"s":"rad","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does the •CF₃ radical attack?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C3"}],"t":"N-Methylpyrrole","p":"Reagents: CF₃SO₂Na, t-BuOOH (•CF₃)","fig":{"sites":2967},"ex":"•CF₃ is an electrophilic radical, so it behaves like an electrophile: it attacks the electron-rich C2 of the pyrrole."},{"s":"rad","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Where does the cyclohexyl radical add?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"β-CH₂"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"α-CH"}],"t":"Methyl acrylate","p":"Reagents: cyclohexyl radical (c-HexI, Bu₃SnH, AIBN)","fig":{"sites":2968},"ex":"Giese addition: the nucleophilic radical adds to the unhindered β-carbon, where the LUMO is largest, and leaves a radical stabilised by the ester."},{"s":"rad","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which C–H does Cl• abstract?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"C2–H (next to O)"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"C3–H"}],"t":"Tetrahydrofuran","p":"Reagents: Cl• (photoredox, Ni–Cl)","fig":{"sites":2969},"ex":"Polarity matching in hydrogen-atom transfer: the electrophilic Cl• abstracts the hydridic C–H next to oxygen, and the nucleophilic α-oxy radical it leaves is then coupled by nickel."},{"s":"rad","k":"Regioselectivity","a":"Which C–H does decatungstate abstract?","o":[{"pre":"C atom","x":"β-C–H"},{"pre":"C atom","x":"α-C–H"}],"t":"Cyclopentanone","p":"Reagents: TBADT (decatungstate), hν","fig":{"sites":2970},"ex":"The α-C–H bonds next to the carbonyl are electron-poor, so the electrophilic excited decatungstate avoids them and abstracts a β-hydrogen, although the α radical would be the more stable one.","h":true}]};</script>
<script>
const DATA = window.CTD_DATA || { apps: {}, mols: [], bank: [] };
const MOLS = DATA.mols, BANK = DATA.bank, APPS = DATA.apps;
const SRC = Object.keys(APPS);
const $ = s => document.querySelector(s);
const esc = s => String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g, ch => ({ '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '"': '&quot;', "'": '&#39;' }[ch]));
const store = {
  get(k) { try { const v = localStorage.getItem(k); return v ? JSON.parse(v) : null; } catch (e) { return null; } },
  set(k, v) { try { localStorage.setItem(k, JSON.stringify(v)); } catch (e) {} }
};
const reduceMotion = matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
const PAGE_URL = 'https://chemtrain.github.io/chemtrain-daily/chemtrain_daily.html';
// copy to the clipboard, or select the text when the browser will not let us
function copyText(text, pre, msg) {
  const fallback = () => { const r = document.createRange(); r.selectNodeContents(pre); const s = getSelection(); s.removeAllRanges(); s.addRange(r); msg.textContent = 'Selected: press Ctrl+C (or ⌘C) to copy.'; };
  try { navigator.clipboard.writeText(text).then(() => { msg.textContent = 'Copied.'; }, fallback); } catch (err) { fallback(); }
}

/* ---------- seeded randomness: the same daily quiz for everyone on the same date ---------- */
function hashStr(s) { let h = 2166136261; for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) { h ^= s.charCodeAt(i); h = Math.imul(h, 16777619); } return h >>> 0; }
function mulberry32(a) { return () => { a |= 0; a = a + 0x6D2B79F5 | 0; let t = Math.imul(a ^ a >>> 15, 1 | a); t = t + Math.imul(t ^ t >>> 7, 61 | t) ^ t; return ((t ^ t >>> 14) >>> 0) / 4294967296; }; }
function shuffleWith(rnd, a) { a = a.slice(); for (let i = a.length - 1; i > 0; i--) { const j = Math.floor(rnd() * (i + 1)); [a[i], a[j]] = [a[j], a[i]]; } return a; }
const shuffle = a => shuffleWith(Math.random, a);

/* ---------- questions ---------- */
const BY_SRC = Object.fromEntries(SRC.map(s => [s, []]));
BANK.forEach((q, i) => { if (BY_SRC[q.s]) BY_SRC[q.s].push(i); });
// x: a question for problem sets only (Reactivity Learn's N/E ones, Photoredox Learn's cycle and electron-transfer ones);
// the daily quiz and Beast mode use the apps that have other questions
const PLAY_SRC = SRC.filter(s => BY_SRC[s].some(i => !BANK[i].x));
const appPill = s => `<span class="pill a-${s}"><i class="adot"></i>${esc(APPS[s].name)}</span>`;
const isSite = q => q.fig && q.fig.sites != null;
const answerOf = q => q.y || 0;
const LETTERS = 'abcd';
const keyOf = (q, j) => isSite(q) ? LETTERS[j] : String(j + 1);

function molSVG(i, maxScale, maxH, maxW, ring, attrs = '') {
  const m = MOLS[i];
  if (!m) return '';
  const [w, h, halos, , body] = m;
  const s = Math.min(maxScale, maxH / h, maxW / w);
  const halo = halos.map(([x, y, r]) => `<circle class="halo" cx="${x}" cy="${y}" r="${r}"/>` + (ring ? `<circle class="ring" cx="${x}" cy="${y}" r="${r}"/>` : '')).join('');
  return `<svg class="mol" viewBox="0 0 ${w} ${h}" width="${Math.round(w * s)}" height="${Math.round(h * s)}" aria-hidden="true"${attrs}>${halo}${body}</svg>`;
}
// lettered candidate atoms; letter j labels option order[j]
function siteSVG(q, order, chosen, maxScale, maxH, maxW) {
  const [w, h, , pts, body] = MOLS[q.fig.sites], done = chosen !== null, BR = 7.5, best = answerOf(q);
  let x0 = 0, y0 = 0, x1 = w, y1 = h, halos = '', marks = '';
  pts.forEach(([x, y, dx, dy, off, r], s) => {
    const j = order.indexOf(s);
    const cls = !done ? ' pick' : s === best ? ' best' : j === chosen ? ' wrong' : ' other';
    const bx = x + dx * off, by = y + dy * off;
    x0 = Math.min(x0, bx - BR - 2); y0 = Math.min(y0, by - BR - 2); x1 = Math.max(x1, bx + BR + 2); y1 = Math.max(y1, by + BR + 2);
    halos += `<circle class="halo${cls}" cx="${x}" cy="${y}" r="${r}"/>` + (done ? '' : `<circle class="ring" cx="${x}" cy="${y}" r="${r}"/>`);
    marks += `<g class="site${cls}" data-j="${j}"><circle class="badge" cx="${bx}" cy="${by}" r="${BR}"/><text class="bl" x="${bx}" y="${by + 3.3}" text-anchor="middle">${LETTERS[j]}</text></g>`;
  });
  const W = x1 - x0, H = y1 - y0, s = Math.min(maxScale, maxH / H, maxW / W);
  return `<svg class="mol smol" viewBox="${x0} ${y0} ${W} ${H}" width="${Math.round(W * s)}" height="${Math.round(H * s)}" role="img" aria-label="Structure with ${pts.length} labelled atoms">${halos}${body}${marks}</svg>`;
}
// key: the part of the conditions that decides the outcome, highlighted
const condHTML = (steps, key = []) => steps.map((s, i) => `<span>${steps.length > 1 ? (i + 1) + '. ' : ''}${
  key.reduce((t, k) => t.split(esc(k)).join(`<b class="key">${esc(k)}</b>`), esc(s)).replace(/\?/g, '<b class="blank">?</b>')}</span>`).join('');
function figureHTML(q, order, chosen) {
  const f = q.fig, done = chosen !== null;
  if (!f) return '';
  if (f.sites != null) return `<figure class="struct">${siteSVG(q, order, chosen, 2.2, 240, 640)}</figure>`;
  if (f.m != null) return `<figure class="struct">${molSVG(f.m, 2.4, 220, 640, !done)}</figure>`;
  // Nucleophile + Electrophile, or with roles Donor → Acceptor (each couple's role under its name)
  if (f.pair) return `<div class="pair">${f.pair.map((m, k) => `<figure class="pcard"><figcaption><p class="eyebrow">${(f.roles || ['Nucleophile', 'Electrophile'])[k]}</p><div class="pname">${esc(f.names[k])}</div>${f.subs ? `<p class="psub">${esc(f.subs[k])}</p>` : ''}</figcaption><div class="pmol">${molSVG(m, 1.6, 140, 300, !done)}</div></figure>`).join(`<div class="plus" aria-hidden="true">${f.roles ? '→' : '+'}</div>`)}</div>`;
  if (f.r) {
    const left = f.r.map((m, k) => molSVG(m, 1.6, 170, 360, false, k === 0 && f.rm != null ? ' data-fig="pg"' : '')).join('<span class="plus" aria-hidden="true">+</span>');
    let right;
    if (f.p != null) right = molSVG(f.p, 1.6, 170, 360);
    else if (done && f.after != null) right = molSVG(f.after, 1.6, 170, 360);
    else if (done && f.veil) right = molSVG(q.o[answerOf(q)].m, 1.6, 170, 360);
    else right = '<span class="qbox" aria-label="Product hidden">?</span>';
    return `<figure class="struct${f.veil && !done ? ' veiled' : ''}" role="img" aria-label="Reaction scheme">${left}<span class="arrow">${condHTML(f.cond || [], f.key)}<i></i></span>${right}</figure>`;
  }
  return '';
}
function optionHTML(q, order, chosen, j) {
  const k = order[j], o = q.o[k], done = chosen !== null;
  let cls = 'opt' + (o.m != null ? ' mol-opt' : '');
  if (done) cls += k === answerOf(q) ? ' correct' : j === chosen ? ' wrong' : ' dim';
  let val;
  if (o.m != null) val = `<span class="val"><span class="othumb">${molSVG(o.m, 1.3, 110, 240, false)}</span>${o.x ? `<span>${esc(o.x)}</span>` : ''}${done && o.v ? `<small>${esc(o.v)}</small>` : ''}</span>`;
  else if (isSite(q)) val = `<span class="val">${esc(done ? o.x : o.pre)}</span>`;
  else val = `<span class="val${/^[−+\d.d⁰¹²³⁴⁵⁶⁷⁸⁹ e]+$/.test(o.x) && o.x.length < 8 ? ' num' : ''}">${esc(o.x)}</span>`;
  return `<button type="button" class="${cls}" data-j="${j}"${done ? ' disabled' : ''}><span class="key">${keyOf(q, j)}</span>${val}</button>`;
}
// what the wrong values were: "16.1, ethanol"
function othersHTML(q) {
  const rest = q.o.filter((o, k) => k !== answerOf(q) && o.w);
  return rest.length ? `<p class="fbline">The other choices: ${rest.map(o => `<strong>${esc(o.x)}</strong> ${esc(o.w)}`).join(' · ')}.</p>` : '';
}
function feedbackHTML(q, order, chosen, extra) {
  const ok = order[chosen] === answerOf(q), a = q.o[answerOf(q)];
  const ansText = a.m != null ? (a.x ? `<strong>${esc(a.x)}</strong> (choice ${keyOf(q, order.indexOf(answerOf(q)))})` : `choice <strong>${keyOf(q, order.indexOf(answerOf(q)))}</strong>`)
    : `<strong>${esc(a.x)}</strong>`;
  const app = APPS[q.s];
  return `<div class="feedback" aria-live="polite">
      <p class="verdict ${ok ? 'good' : 'bad'}">${ok ? 'Correct' : 'Not quite'}</p>
      ${ok && a.m == null ? '' : `<p class="fbline">The answer is ${ansText}.</p>`}
      ${q.ex ? `<p class="note">${esc(q.ex)}</p>` : ''}
      ${othersHTML(q)}
      ${q.mc && q.mc.length ? `<div class="mech"><p class="mech-h">Mechanism</p><ol>${q.mc.map(t => `<li>${esc(t)}</li>`).join('')}</ol></div>` : ''}
      ${(q.lk || []).map(g => `<p class="src">${esc(g.h)}: ${g.l.map(([t, u]) => `<a href="${esc(u)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${esc(t)} ↗</a>`).join(' · ')}</p>`).join('')}
      <p class="src">From <a href="${esc(app.page)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${esc(app.name)}</a>: practise more there.</p>
      ${extra}
    </div>`;
}
function questionHTML(q, order, chosen, eyebrow, extraTags, after) {
  const tags = appPill(q.s) + (q.h ? '<span class="pill hard">Hard</span>' : '') + (extraTags || '');
  return `<div class="panel">
      <div class="qhead"><div class="qtop"><p class="eyebrow">${eyebrow}</p><div class="tags">${tags}</div></div>
        <p class="eyebrow">${esc(q.k)}</p>
        ${q.t ? `<h2 class="term">${esc(q.t)}</h2>` : ''}${q.f ? `<p class="formula">${esc(q.f)}</p>` : ''}${q.p ? `<p class="partner">${esc(q.p)}</p>` : ''}
        ${figureHTML(q, order, chosen)}
        <p class="ask">${esc(q.a)}${chosen === null && isSite(q) ? ' Pick its letter on the structure or below.' : ''}</p></div>
      <div class="options">${order.map((_, j) => optionHTML(q, order, chosen, j)).join('')}</div>
      ${chosen !== null ? feedbackHTML(q, order, chosen, after || '') : ''}
    </div>`;
}
// once answered, PG Learn's removed group glows in the starting material
function markRemoved(box, q, chosen) {
  if (chosen === null || !q.fig || q.fig.rm == null) return;
  const g = box.querySelector(`svg[data-fig="pg"] [data-s="${q.fig.rm}"]`);
  if (g) g.classList.add('rm');
}

/* ---------- daily ---------- */
const PER_APP = 2;
const FIRST_DAY = '2026-10-21';
const pad = n => String(n).padStart(2, '0');
const dateKey = d => `${d.getFullYear()}-${pad(d.getMonth() + 1)}-${pad(d.getDate())}`;
const dayNumber = key => Math.round((Date.parse(key + 'T12:00:00') - Date.parse(FIRST_DAY + 'T12:00:00')) / 864e5) + 1;
const longDate = key => new Date(key + 'T12:00:00').toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { weekday: 'long', day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric' });
// Two questions from each app (Hard ones left out), in a seeded order, with seeded choice orders.
function dailyQuiz(key) {
  const rnd = mulberry32(hashStr('chemtrain-daily|' + key));
  let picks = [];
  for (const s of PLAY_SRC) {
    const pool = BY_SRC[s].filter(i => !BANK[i].h && !BANK[i].x);
    picks.push(...shuffleWith(rnd, pool).slice(0, PER_APP));
  }
  picks = shuffleWith(rnd, picks);
  return picks.map(i => ({ i, order: shuffleWith(rnd, BANK[i].o.map((_, k) => k)) }));
}
const today = dateKey(new Date());
const history = store.get('ctd.history') || {};  // date -> score
const dy = { key: today, quiz: dailyQuiz(today), answers: (store.get('ctd.daily.' + today) || {}).answers || [], started: false };
dy.started = dy.answers.length > 0;
const dyDone = () => dy.answers.length >= dy.quiz.length;
const dyRight = () => dy.answers.filter((a, n) => a !== null && dy.quiz[n].order[a] === answerOf(BANK[dy.quiz[n].i])).length;
let dyShowing = dy.answers.length;  // the question on screen; equals answers.length while it waits for an answer
let reviewMode = false;              // looking back at a question from the results

function dailyAnswer(j) {
  if (dyDone() || dyShowing !== dy.answers.length) return;
  dy.answers.push(j);
  store.set('ctd.daily.' + today, { answers: dy.answers });
  if (dyDone()) { history[today] = dyRight(); store.set('ctd.history', history); }
  renderDaily(); renderDailySide();
  const b = $('#dy-next'); if (b) b.focus({ preventScroll: true });
}
function dotsHTML() {
  return `<div class="dots" aria-hidden="true">${dy.quiz.map((it, n) => {
    if (n < dy.answers.length) return `<i class="${it.order[dy.answers[n]] === answerOf(BANK[it.i]) ? 'ok' : 'no'}"></i>`;
    return `<i class="${n === dyShowing ? 'now' : ''}"></i>`;
  }).join('')}</div>`;
}
function shareText() {
  const squares = dy.quiz.map((it, n) => it.order[dy.answers[n]] === answerOf(BANK[it.i]) ? '🟩' : '🟥').join('');
  return `ChemTrain Daily #${dayNumber(today)} · ${dyRight()}/${dy.quiz.length}\n${squares}\n${PAGE_URL}`;
}
function renderDaily() {
  const el = $('#daily-box'), n = dy.quiz.length;
  if (!dy.started) {
    el.innerHTML = `<div class="panel">
        <div class="qhead"><p class="eyebrow">Daily quiz #${dayNumber(today)}</p><h2 class="term">${esc(longDate(today))}</h2>
        <p class="ask">${n} questions, two from each of these ChemTrain apps. Everyone gets the same ${n} today, and a new quiz starts at midnight.</p></div>
        <div class="tags">${PLAY_SRC.map(appPill).join('')}</div>
        <div class="row end"><button type="button" class="btn" id="dy-start">Start today's quiz</button></div>
      </div>`;
    return;
  }
  if (dyDone() && dyShowing >= n) {
    const right = dyRight();
    const rows = dy.quiz.map((it, k) => {
      const q = BANK[it.i], ok = it.order[dy.answers[k]] === answerOf(q);
      return `<li><span class="adot a-${q.s}" title="${esc(APPS[q.s].name)}"></span><span class="t">${esc(q.t || q.k)}<small>${esc(APPS[q.s].name)} · ${esc(q.k)}</small></span>
        <button type="button" class="btn ghost small" data-review="${k}">${ok ? '✓ Review' : '✗ Review'}</button></li>`;
    }).join('');
    el.innerHTML = `<div class="panel">
        <div class="qhead"><p class="eyebrow">Daily quiz #${dayNumber(today)} · done</p>
          <p class="score">${right}<small> / ${n}</small></p>
          <p class="ask">${right === n ? 'A perfect day.' : right >= 7 ? 'Nicely done.' : 'Each answer has its explanation; review the ones you missed.'} A new quiz starts at midnight.</p></div>
        ${dotsHTML()}
        <ul class="results">${rows}</ul>
        <div class="stack"><p class="eyebrow">Share your result</p><pre class="share" id="share-text">${esc(shareText())}</pre>
          <div class="row between"><span class="msg" id="share-msg" role="status"></span><button type="button" class="btn" id="dy-share">Copy result</button></div></div>
        <div class="row end"><button type="button" class="btn ghost" data-goto="beast">Try Beast mode</button></div>
      </div>`;
    return;
  }
  const it = dy.quiz[dyShowing], q = BANK[it.i];
  const chosen = dyShowing < dy.answers.length ? dy.answers[dyShowing] : null;
  const nextLabel = dyShowing + 1 < n ? 'Next question' : 'See your score';
  const reviewing = dyDone() && dy.answers.length === n && history[today] !== undefined && dyShowing < n && reviewMode;
  const after = chosen !== null ? `<div class="row end">${reviewing ? '<button type="button" class="btn ghost" id="dy-results">Back to results</button>' : ''}<button type="button" class="btn" id="dy-next">${nextLabel}</button></div>` : '';
  el.innerHTML = `<div class="stack">${dotsHTML()}${questionHTML(q, it.order, chosen, `Daily #${dayNumber(today)} · ${dyShowing + 1} of ${n}`, '', after)}</div>`;
  markRemoved(el, q, chosen);
}
$('#daily-box').addEventListener('click', e => {
  const t = e.target.closest('[data-j]');
  if (t && !t.disabled) return dailyAnswer(+t.dataset.j);
  const b = e.target.closest('button');
  if (!b) return;
  if (b.id === 'dy-start') { dy.started = true; dyShowing = 0; renderDaily(); }
  else if (b.id === 'dy-next') { dyShowing++; renderDaily(); scrollMain(); }
  else if (b.id === 'dy-results') { dyShowing = dy.quiz.length; reviewMode = false; renderDaily(); }
  else if (b.dataset.review !== undefined) { dyShowing = +b.dataset.review; reviewMode = true; renderDaily(); scrollMain(); }
  else if (b.id === 'dy-share') copyText(shareText(), $('#share-text'), $('#share-msg'));
  else if (b.dataset.goto) show(b.dataset.goto);
});
function renderDailySide() {
  const days = Object.keys(history).sort();
  const scores = days.map(d => history[d]);
  // consecutive days with a finished quiz, counting back from today (or yesterday, while today is still open)
  let streak = 0;
  const d = new Date(today + 'T12:00:00');
  if (history[today] === undefined) d.setDate(d.getDate() - 1);
  while (history[dateKey(d)] !== undefined) { streak++; d.setDate(d.getDate() - 1); }
  const week = [];
  for (let k = 6; k >= 0; k--) {
    const d = new Date(today + 'T12:00:00'); d.setDate(d.getDate() - k);
    const key = dateKey(d), s = history[key];
    week.push(`<div><i class="${s !== undefined ? 'played' : ''}${key === today ? ' today' : ''}" title="${esc(longDate(key))}">${s !== undefined ? s : ''}</i>${d.toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { weekday: 'narrow' })}</div>`);
  }
  const avg = scores.length ? (scores.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / scores.length).toFixed(1) : '–';
  $('#daily-side').innerHTML = `<div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b>${streak}</b><span>day${streak === 1 ? '' : 's'} in a row</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>${days.length}</b><span>${days.length === 1 ? 'quiz' : 'quizzes'} played</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>${scores.length ? Math.max(...scores) : '–'}</b><span>best score</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>${avg}</b><span>average</span></div></div>
    <div class="week">${week.join('')}</div>
    <p>Scores are saved in this browser.</p>`;
}

/* ---------- beast mode ---------- */
const POOLS = ['all', ...PLAY_SRC];
const poolName = p => p === 'all' ? 'All apps' : APPS[p].name;
const poolSize = p => (p === 'all' ? PLAY_SRC.flatMap(s => BY_SRC[s]) : BY_SRC[p]).filter(i => !BANK[i].x).length;
const HARD_FROM = 10;  // Hard questions join the run after this many right answers
const bests = store.get('ctd.beast') || {};
const bx = { pool: store.get('ctd.beast.pool') || 'all', phase: 'start', used: new Set(), score: 0, cur: null, newBest: false, stopped: null };
if (!POOLS.includes(bx.pool)) bx.pool = 'all';
function beastNext() {
  const srcs = bx.pool === 'all' ? PLAY_SRC : [bx.pool];
  const fits = i => !bx.used.has(i) && !BANK[i].x && (bx.score >= HARD_FROM || !BANK[i].h);
  // pick the app first, so every app comes up as often in "All apps"
  const open = shuffle(srcs).filter(s => BY_SRC[s].some(fits));
  if (!open.length) { bx.phase = 'cleared'; return renderBeast(); }
  const pool = BY_SRC[open[0]].filter(fits), i = pool[Math.floor(Math.random() * pool.length)];
  bx.used.add(i);
  bx.cur = { i, order: shuffle(BANK[i].o.map((_, k) => k)), chosen: null };
  bx.phase = 'play';
  renderBeast();
}
function beastStart() { bx.used = new Set(); bx.score = 0; bx.newBest = false; bx.stopped = null; beastNext(); scrollMain(); }
function keepBest() {
  if (bx.score > (bests[bx.pool] || 0)) { bests[bx.pool] = bx.score; bx.newBest = true; store.set('ctd.beast', bests); }
}
// leaving a run part-way ends it there; the streak so far still counts
function beastChoose(p) {
  if (bx.phase === 'play') { keepBest(); bx.stopped = bx.score ? { pool: bx.pool, score: bx.score, best: bx.newBest } : null; }
  else if (bx.phase !== 'start') bx.stopped = null;
  bx.pool = p; store.set('ctd.beast.pool', p); bx.phase = 'start';
  renderBeast(); renderBeastSide();
}
function beastAnswer(j) {
  const c = bx.cur;
  if (bx.phase !== 'play' || !c || c.chosen !== null) return;
  c.chosen = j;
  if (c.order[j] === answerOf(BANK[c.i])) bx.score++;
  else {
    bx.phase = 'over';
    keepBest();
  }
  renderBeast(); renderBeastSide();
  const b = $('#bx-next') || $('#bx-again'); if (b) b.focus({ preventScroll: true });
}
function renderBeast() {
  const el = $('#beast-box');
  if (bx.phase === 'start') {
    el.innerHTML = `<div class="panel">
        <div class="qhead"><p class="eyebrow">Beast mode</p><h2 class="term">How far can you go?</h2>
        <p class="ask">Questions keep coming until your first mistake; your score is how many you get right in a row. Harder questions join after ${HARD_FROM}. Choose what to play:</p>
        ${bx.stopped ? `<p class="fbline">You stopped your ${esc(poolName(bx.stopped.pool))} run at <strong>${bx.stopped.score} in a row</strong>${bx.stopped.best ? ', a new best' : ''}.</p>` : ''}</div>
        <div class="pools" role="group" aria-label="Questions from">${POOLS.map(p => `<button type="button" class="pool a-${p}" data-pool="${p}" aria-pressed="${p === bx.pool}">
            <span class="nm"><i class="adot"></i>${esc(poolName(p))}</span>
            <span class="meta">${poolSize(p)} questions · best ${bests[p] || 0}</span></button>`).join('')}</div>
        <div class="row end"><button type="button" class="btn" id="bx-go">Start: ${esc(poolName(bx.pool))}</button></div>
      </div>`;
    return;
  }
  if (bx.phase === 'cleared') {
    el.innerHTML = `<div class="panel"><div class="qhead"><p class="eyebrow">Beast mode · ${esc(poolName(bx.pool))}</p><h2 class="term">You answered every question</h2>
      <p class="ask">${bx.score} in a row without a mistake. There is nothing left to ask.</p></div>
      <div class="row end"><button type="button" class="btn" id="bx-change">Choose again</button></div></div>`;
    return;
  }
  const c = bx.cur, q = BANK[c.i], best = Math.max(bests[bx.pool] || 0, bx.score);
  let after = '';
  if (c.chosen !== null) {
    after = bx.phase === 'over'
      ? `<div class="over${bx.newBest ? ' best' : ''}"><h3>${bx.newBest ? `New best: ${bx.score} in a row` : `Game over: ${bx.score} in a row`}</h3>
          <p class="fbline">Your best in ${esc(poolName(bx.pool))}: <strong>${best}</strong>.</p></div>
          <div class="row end"><button type="button" class="btn ghost" id="bx-change">Choose again</button><button type="button" class="btn" id="bx-again">Play again</button></div>`
      : `<div class="row end"><button type="button" class="btn" id="bx-next">Next question</button></div>`;
  }
  el.innerHTML = `<div class="stack">
      <div class="streak"><b>${bx.score}</b><span>in a row · best ${best} · ${esc(poolName(bx.pool))}</span></div>
      ${questionHTML(q, c.order, c.chosen, `Beast mode · question ${bx.score + (c.chosen !== null && bx.phase === 'play' ? 0 : 1)}`, '', after)}</div>`;
  markRemoved(el, q, c.chosen);
}
$('#beast-box').addEventListener('click', e => {
  const t = e.target.closest('[data-j]');
  if (t && !t.disabled) return beastAnswer(+t.dataset.j);
  const b = e.target.closest('button');
  if (!b) return;
  if (b.dataset.pool) beastChoose(b.dataset.pool);
  else if (b.id === 'bx-go' || b.id === 'bx-again') beastStart();
  else if (b.id === 'bx-next') { beastNext(); scrollMain(); }
  else if (b.id === 'bx-change') beastChoose(bx.pool);
});
function renderBeastSide() {
  const best = p => Math.max(bests[p] || 0, p === bx.pool && bx.phase === 'play' ? bx.score : 0);
  $('#beast-side').innerHTML = `<div class="bests" role="group" aria-label="Play">${POOLS.map(p => `<button type="button" data-pool="${p}" aria-pressed="${p === bx.pool}"><span class="pill a-${p}"><i class="adot"></i>${esc(poolName(p))}</span><b>${best(p)}</b></button>`).join('')}</div>
    <p>Your longest run without a mistake in each pool, saved in this browser. Pick one to play it; a run you leave ends there.</p>`;
}
$('#beast-side').addEventListener('click', e => {
  const b = e.target.closest('button[data-pool]');
  if (!b) return;
  beastChoose(b.dataset.pool);
  scrollMain();
  const go = $('#bx-go'); if (go) go.focus({ preventScroll: true });
});

/* ---------- lazy problem set ---------- */
// a topic is one kind of question from one app, e.g. pKa Learn · pKa value
const TOPICS = [];
{
  const byId = new Map();
  BANK.forEach((q, i) => {
    const id = q.s + '|' + q.k;
    if (!byId.has(id)) byId.set(id, { id, s: q.s, k: q.k, idx: [] });
    byId.get(id).idx.push(i);
  });
  for (const s of SRC) for (const t of byId.values()) if (t.s === s) TOPICS.push(t);
}
const TOPIC = Object.fromEntries(TOPICS.map(t => [t.id, t]));
const onlyHere = t => t.idx.every(i => BANK[i].x);
const PRESETS = [50, 100, 150];
// A saved or shared set names its questions by content (everything but the drawing numbers), so it survives a
// rebuild that renumbers the bank; a question whose text changed drops out.
const qSig = q => hashStr(JSON.stringify([q.s, q.k, q.a, q.t || '', q.f || '', q.p || '', q.ex || '',
  q.o.map(o => [o.x || '', o.v || '', o.w || '', o.pre || '']), q.fig ? [q.fig.cond || '', q.fig.names || '', q.fig.key || ''] : ''])).toString(36);
const BY_SIG = new Map();
BANK.forEach((q, i) => { const h = qSig(q); if (!BY_SIG.has(h)) BY_SIG.set(h, i); });

const setPrefs = Object.assign({ topics: TOPICS.map(t => t.id), n: 50, hard: true }, store.get('ctd.set.prefs') || {});
setPrefs.topics = setPrefs.topics.filter(id => TOPIC[id]);
const psFits = i => setPrefs.hard || !BANK[i].h;
const topicCount = id => TOPIC[id].idx.filter(psFits).length;
const available = () => setPrefs.topics.reduce((a, id) => a + topicCount(id), 0);
const savePrefs = () => store.set('ctd.set.prefs', setPrefs);

function loadSet() {
  const saved = store.get('ctd.set');
  if (!saved || !Array.isArray(saved.items)) return null;
  const items = [], answers = [];
  saved.items.forEach(([h, order], n) => {
    const i = BY_SIG.get(h);
    if (i === undefined || order.length !== BANK[i].o.length) return;  // that question changed in a later build
    items.push({ i, order });
    if (n < saved.answers.length) answers.push(saved.answers[n]);
  });
  return items.length ? { ...saved, items, answers } : null;
}
function saveSet() {
  if (!ps) { try { localStorage.removeItem('ctd.set'); } catch (e) {} return; }
  store.set('ctd.set', { ...ps, items: ps.items.map(it => [qSig(BANK[it.i]), it.order]) });
}
let ps = loadSet();                         // the set being played; null on the setup screen
let psShowing = ps ? ps.answers.length : 0; // the question on screen
let psReview = false;                       // looking back at a question from the results
const psDone = () => ps.ended || ps.answers.length >= ps.items.length;
const psOk = n => ps.items[n].order[ps.answers[n]] === answerOf(BANK[ps.items[n].i]);
const psRight = () => ps.answers.filter((a, n) => psOk(n)).length;

// Every set has a seed. The choice order of each question follows from the seed and the question, so a
// shared link only has to carry the seed and the questions' signatures.
const newSeed = () => Math.floor(Math.random() * 2 ** 32).toString(36);
const orderFor = (seed, i) => shuffleWith(mulberry32(hashStr(seed + '|' + qSig(BANK[i]))), BANK[i].o.map((_, k) => k));
// Deal the questions round the chosen topics, one each in turn, so every topic gets an equal share
// (a topic that runs out drops out); then shuffle the whole set.
function drawSet(topics, n, seed) {
  const rnd = mulberry32(hashStr('set|' + seed));
  const pools = topics.map(id => shuffleWith(rnd, TOPIC[id].idx.filter(psFits)));
  const picks = [];
  while (picks.length < n && pools.some(p => p.length)) {
    for (const p of shuffleWith(rnd, pools)) { if (picks.length >= n) break; if (p.length) picks.push(p.pop()); }
  }
  return shuffleWith(rnd, picks).map(i => ({ i, order: orderFor(seed, i) }));
}
function startSet(items, seed, kind) {
  ps = { items, answers: [], seed, kind, date: today, ended: false, logged: false };
  psShowing = 0; psReview = false; psShare = false;
  saveSet(); renderSet(); renderSetSide(); scrollMain();
}
const setName = () => ({ retry: 'Retry set', shared: 'Shared set' }[ps.kind] || 'Problem set');
const setLink = () => `${PAGE_URL}#ps=${ps.seed}.${ps.items.map(it => qSig(BANK[it.i])).join('.')}`;
const topicTotal = items => new Set(items.map(it => BANK[it.i].s + '|' + BANK[it.i].k)).size;
function setShareText(withScore) {
  const total = ps.items.length, answered = ps.answers.length, t = topicTotal(ps.items);
  return withScore
    ? `ChemTrain problem set · ${psRight()}/${answered}${answered < total ? ` (stopped after ${answered} of ${total})` : ''}\nThe same ${total} questions for you: ${setLink()}`
    : `ChemTrain problem set · ${total} question${total === 1 ? '' : 's'} from ${t} topic${t === 1 ? '' : 's'}\n${setLink()}`;
}
const shareBoxHTML = text => `<div class="stack"><pre class="share long" id="ps-share-text">${esc(text)}</pre>
    <div class="row between"><span class="msg" id="ps-share-msg" role="status"></span><button type="button" class="btn small" id="ps-copy">Copy</button></div></div>`;
// a link from someone else: #ps=<seed>.<signature>.<signature>...
function parseShared(hash) {
  const m = /^#?ps=([0-9a-z]+)((?:\.[0-9a-z]+)+)$/.exec(hash || '');
  if (!m) return null;
  const seed = m[1], sigs = m[2].slice(1).split('.'), seen = new Set(), items = [];
  for (const h of sigs) {
    const i = BY_SIG.get(h);
    if (i !== undefined && !seen.has(i)) { seen.add(i); items.push({ i, order: orderFor(seed, i) }); }
  }
  return items.length ? { seed, items, lost: sigs.length - items.length } : null;
}
let shared = parseShared(location.hash);
let psShare = false;  // the share box on the question screen
// window.history: the page's own "history" is the daily scores
function clearHash() { try { window.history.replaceState(null, '', location.href.split('#')[0]); } catch (e) {} }
function logSet() {
  if (ps.logged || !ps.answers.length) return;
  ps.logged = true;
  const log = store.get('ctd.sets') || [];
  log.push({ d: ps.date, n: ps.answers.length, r: psRight(), kind: ps.kind });
  store.set('ctd.sets', log.slice(-50));
}
function psAnswer(j) {
  if (!ps || psDone() || psShowing !== ps.answers.length) return;
  ps.answers.push(j);
  if (psDone()) logSet();
  saveSet(); renderSet(); renderSetSide();
  const b = $('#ps-next'); if (b) b.focus({ preventScroll: true });
}

function renderSetup() {
  const sel = new Set(setPrefs.topics), avail = available();
  const groups = SRC.map(s => {
    const ts = TOPICS.filter(t => t.s === s), all = ts.every(t => sel.has(t.id));
    return `<div class="tgroup"><div class="tgroup-h"><span class="nm"><i class="adot a-${s}"></i>${esc(APPS[s].name)}</span>
        <button type="button" class="linkbtn" data-app="${s}">${all ? 'None' : 'All'}</button></div>
      <div class="chips">${ts.map(t => `<button type="button" class="chip${onlyHere(t) ? ' only' : ''}" data-topic="${esc(t.id)}" aria-pressed="${sel.has(t.id)}"${onlyHere(t) ? ' title="Only in problem sets"' : ''}>${esc(t.k)}<small>${topicCount(t.id)}</small></button>`).join('')}</div></div>`;
  }).join('');
  const hardN = TOPICS.reduce((a, t) => a + t.idx.filter(i => BANK[i].h).length, 0);
  $('#set-box').innerHTML = `<div class="panel">
      <div class="qhead"><p class="eyebrow">Lazy problem set</p><h2 class="term">Pick the topics, get the questions</h2>
        <p class="ask">Choose any mix of topics and how many questions you want. They are drawn at random and shared equally between your topics. Your progress is saved after every answer, so you can stop and come back.</p></div>
      <div class="field"><div class="row between"><p class="field-h">Topics</p>
          <div class="row"><button type="button" class="linkbtn" id="ps-all">Select all</button><button type="button" class="linkbtn" id="ps-none">Clear all</button></div></div>
        <div class="topics">${groups}</div>
        <p class="help">Topics with a dashed border are only in problem sets, never in the daily quiz or Beast mode.</p></div>
      <div class="field"><p class="field-h">Difficulty</p>
        <div class="chips"><button type="button" class="chip" id="ps-hard" aria-pressed="${setPrefs.hard}">Include Hard questions<small>${hardN}</small></button></div></div>
      <div class="field"><p class="field-h" id="ps-n-h">How many questions?</p>
        <div class="row" role="group" aria-labelledby="ps-n-h">${PRESETS.map(n => `<button type="button" class="chip" data-n="${n}" aria-pressed="false">${n}</button>`).join('')}
          <label class="row" style="gap:8px"><span class="help">or</span><input class="count" id="ps-n" type="number" inputmode="numeric" min="1" max="${Math.max(avail, 1)}" step="1" value="${setPrefs.n}" aria-label="Number of questions"></label></div>
        <p class="help" id="ps-note" role="status"></p></div>
      <div class="row end"><button type="button" class="btn" id="ps-go"></button></div>
    </div>`;
  updateCount();
}
// the parts that follow the number box, updated as you type without redrawing the box
function updateCount() {
  const avail = available(), want = setPrefs.n, n = Math.min(want, avail);
  document.querySelectorAll('#set-box [data-n]').forEach(b => b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(+b.dataset.n === want)));
  const go = $('#ps-go'), note = $('#ps-note');
  go.disabled = !(n >= 1);
  go.textContent = n >= 1 ? `Draw ${n} question${n === 1 ? '' : 's'}` : 'Draw questions';
  note.textContent = !setPrefs.topics.length ? 'Pick at least one topic.'
    : !(want >= 1) ? 'Type how many questions you want.'
    : want > avail ? `Your topics have ${avail} questions, so the set will have all ${avail}.`
    : `${avail} questions in your topics.`;
}
function renderShared() {
  const items = shared.items, t = new Map();
  items.forEach(it => { const q = BANK[it.i], id = q.s + '|' + q.k; t.set(id, (t.get(id) || 0) + 1); });
  const pills = [...t].sort((a, b) => TOPICS.indexOf(TOPIC[a[0]]) - TOPICS.indexOf(TOPIC[b[0]]))
    .map(([id, n]) => `<span class="pill a-${TOPIC[id].s}" title="${esc(APPS[TOPIC[id].s].name)}"><i class="adot"></i>${esc(TOPIC[id].k)} · ${n}</span>`).join('');
  const busy = ps && !psDone();
  $('#set-box').innerHTML = `<div class="panel">
      <div class="qhead"><p class="eyebrow">Shared problem set</p><h2 class="term">${items.length} question${items.length === 1 ? '' : 's'} from ${t.size} topic${t.size === 1 ? '' : 's'}</h2>
        <p class="ask">Someone sent you this set. Everyone who opens the link gets the same questions in the same order, with the same choices, so you can compare scores.</p></div>
      <div class="tags">${pills}</div>
      ${shared.lost ? `<p class="help">${shared.lost} of its questions have changed since the link was made and are left out.</p>` : ''}
      ${busy ? `<p class="help">Starting it replaces the set you have in progress (${ps.answers.length} of ${ps.items.length} answered).</p>` : ''}
      <div class="row end"><button type="button" class="btn ghost" id="ps-skip">Not now</button><button type="button" class="btn" id="ps-take">Start this set</button></div>
    </div>`;
}
function renderSet() {
  const el = $('#set-box');
  if (shared) return renderShared();
  if (!ps) return renderSetup();
  const total = ps.items.length, answered = ps.answers.length, right = psRight();
  if (psDone() && !psReview) {
    const byTopic = new Map();
    ps.answers.forEach((a, n) => {
      const q = BANK[ps.items[n].i], id = q.s + '|' + q.k;
      if (!byTopic.has(id)) byTopic.set(id, { s: q.s, k: q.k, n: 0, r: 0 });
      const t = byTopic.get(id); t.n++; if (psOk(n)) t.r++;
    });
    const topics = [...byTopic.values()].sort((a, b) => SRC.indexOf(a.s) - SRC.indexOf(b.s) || a.r / a.n - b.r / b.n);
    const missed = ps.answers.map((a, n) => n).filter(n => !psOk(n));
    const pct = answered ? Math.round(100 * right / answered) : 0;
    el.innerHTML = `<div class="panel">
        <div class="qhead"><p class="eyebrow">${setName()} · done</p>
          <p class="score">${right}<small> / ${answered}</small></p>
          <p class="ask">${pct}% right${answered < total ? `; you stopped after ${answered} of ${total} questions` : ''}. ${missed.length ? 'Each answer has its explanation: review the ones you missed below.' : 'Not a single mistake.'}</p></div>
        <div class="stack"><p class="eyebrow">By topic</p>
          <ul class="results">${topics.map(t => `<li><span class="adot a-${t.s}" title="${esc(APPS[t.s].name)}"></span><span class="t">${esc(t.k)}<small>${esc(APPS[t.s].name)}</small></span><b>${t.r} / ${t.n}</b></li>`).join('')}</ul></div>
        ${missed.length ? `<div class="stack"><p class="eyebrow">Missed (${missed.length})</p>
          <ul class="results">${missed.map(n => { const q = BANK[ps.items[n].i]; return `<li><span class="adot a-${q.s}" title="${esc(APPS[q.s].name)}"></span><span class="t">${esc(q.t || q.k)}<small>${esc(APPS[q.s].name)} · ${esc(q.k)}</small></span>
            <button type="button" class="btn ghost small" data-review="${n}">Review</button></li>`; }).join('')}</ul></div>` : ''}
        <div class="stack"><p class="eyebrow">Compare with friends</p>
          <p class="help">Send this to others: the link gives them the same questions, and your score to beat.</p>${shareBoxHTML(setShareText(true))}</div>
        <div class="row end">${missed.length ? `<button type="button" class="btn ghost" id="ps-retry">Retry the ${missed.length} you missed</button>` : ''}<button type="button" class="btn" id="ps-new">New problem set</button></div>
      </div>`;
    return;
  }
  const it = ps.items[psShowing], q = BANK[it.i];
  const chosen = psShowing < answered ? ps.answers[psShowing] : null;
  let after = '';
  if (chosen !== null) after = psReview
    ? '<div class="row end"><button type="button" class="btn" id="ps-results">Back to results</button></div>'
    : `<div class="row end">${psShowing + 1 < total ? '<button type="button" class="btn ghost" id="ps-end">End the set here</button>' : ''}<button type="button" class="btn" id="ps-next">${psShowing + 1 < total ? 'Next question' : 'See your results'}</button></div>`;
  el.innerHTML = `<div class="stack">
      <div class="setline"><span>${answered} of ${total} answered · ${right} right</span>${psReview ? '' : `<button type="button" class="linkbtn" id="ps-share" aria-expanded="${psShare}">${psShare ? 'Hide the link' : 'Share this set'}</button>`}</div>
      <div class="progress" aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:${(100 * answered / total).toFixed(1)}%"></i></div>
      ${psShare && !psReview ? `<div class="panel"><p class="help">Anyone who opens this link gets the same ${total} questions, in the same order, with the same choices.</p>${shareBoxHTML(setShareText(false))}</div>` : ''}
      ${questionHTML(q, it.order, chosen, `${setName()} · ${psShowing + 1} of ${total}`, '', after)}</div>`;
  markRemoved(el, q, chosen);
}
$('#set-box').addEventListener('click', e => {
  const t = e.target.closest('[data-j]');
  if (t && !t.disabled) return psAnswer(+t.dataset.j);
  const b = e.target.closest('button');
  if (!b) return;
  const sel = new Set(setPrefs.topics);
  const setTopics = ids => { setPrefs.topics = TOPICS.map(t => t.id).filter(id => ids.has(id)); savePrefs(); renderSetup(); };
  if (b.dataset.topic) { sel.has(b.dataset.topic) ? sel.delete(b.dataset.topic) : sel.add(b.dataset.topic); setTopics(sel); }
  else if (b.dataset.app) {
    const ts = TOPICS.filter(t => t.s === b.dataset.app), all = ts.every(t => sel.has(t.id));
    ts.forEach(t => all ? sel.delete(t.id) : sel.add(t.id)); setTopics(sel);
  }
  else if (b.id === 'ps-all') setTopics(new Set(TOPICS.map(t => t.id)));
  else if (b.id === 'ps-none') setTopics(new Set());
  else if (b.id === 'ps-hard') { setPrefs.hard = !setPrefs.hard; savePrefs(); renderSetup(); }
  else if (b.dataset.n) { setPrefs.n = +b.dataset.n; savePrefs(); $('#ps-n').value = setPrefs.n; updateCount(); }
  else if (b.id === 'ps-go') { const n = Math.min(setPrefs.n, available()), seed = newSeed(); if (n >= 1) startSet(drawSet(setPrefs.topics, n, seed), seed, 'new'); }
  else if (b.id === 'ps-next') { psShowing++; renderSet(); scrollMain(); }
  else if (b.id === 'ps-end') { ps.ended = true; logSet(); saveSet(); renderSet(); renderSetSide(); scrollMain(); }
  else if (b.id === 'ps-results') { psReview = false; renderSet(); scrollMain(); }
  else if (b.dataset.review !== undefined) { psShowing = +b.dataset.review; psReview = true; renderSet(); scrollMain(); }
  else if (b.id === 'ps-retry') {
    const missed = ps.answers.map((a, n) => n).filter(n => !psOk(n)).map(n => ps.items[n].i);
    const seed = newSeed();
    startSet(shuffleWith(mulberry32(hashStr('set|' + seed)), missed).map(i => ({ i, order: orderFor(seed, i) })), seed, 'retry');
  }
  else if (b.id === 'ps-new') { ps = null; saveSet(); renderSet(); renderSetSide(); scrollMain(); }
  else if (b.id === 'ps-share') { psShare = !psShare; renderSet(); }
  else if (b.id === 'ps-copy') copyText($('#ps-share-text').textContent, $('#ps-share-text'), $('#ps-share-msg'));
  else if (b.id === 'ps-take') { const sh = shared; shared = null; clearHash(); startSet(sh.items, sh.seed, 'shared'); }
  else if (b.id === 'ps-skip') { shared = null; clearHash(); renderSet(); renderSetSide(); }
});
$('#set-box').addEventListener('input', e => {
  if (e.target.id !== 'ps-n') return;
  setPrefs.n = Math.floor(+e.target.value); savePrefs(); updateCount();
});
$('#set-box').addEventListener('keydown', e => { if (e.target.id === 'ps-n' && e.key === 'Enter') { e.preventDefault(); $('#ps-go').click(); } });
let discardArmed = 0;
function renderSetSide() {
  const log = store.get('ctd.sets') || [];
  const n = log.reduce((a, x) => a + x.n, 0), r = log.reduce((a, x) => a + x.r, 0);
  let cur = '';
  if (ps && !psDone()) {
    const total = ps.items.length, answered = ps.answers.length;
    cur = `<div class="stack" style="gap:8px"><p class="eyebrow">${setName()} in progress</p>
        <div class="progress" aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:${(100 * answered / total).toFixed(1)}%"></i></div>
        <div class="setline"><span>${answered} of ${total} answered</span><span>${psRight()} right</span></div>
        <div class="row"><button type="button" class="btn ghost small" id="ps-discard">${discardArmed ? 'Click again to discard' : 'Discard this set'}</button></div></div>`;
  }
  const recent = log.slice(-5).reverse().map(x => `<div><span>${esc(new Date(x.d + 'T12:00:00').toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'short' }))} · ${x.n} question${x.n === 1 ? '' : 's'}${x.kind === 'retry' ? ' (retry)' : x.kind === 'shared' ? ' (shared)' : ''}</span><b>${x.r}/${x.n}</b></div>`).join('');
  $('#set-side').innerHTML = `${cur}<div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b>${log.length}</b><span>set${log.length === 1 ? '' : 's'} finished</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>${n ? Math.round(100 * r / n) + '%' : '–'}</b><span>${n ? `right of ${n} answered` : 'right so far'}</span></div></div>
    ${recent ? `<div class="bests">${recent}</div>` : ''}
    <p>Saved in this browser.</p>`;
}
$('#set-side').addEventListener('click', e => {
  if (!e.target.closest('#ps-discard')) return;
  if (!discardArmed) { discardArmed = setTimeout(() => { discardArmed = 0; renderSetSide(); }, 4000); return renderSetSide(); }
  clearTimeout(discardArmed); discardArmed = 0;
  ps = null; saveSet(); renderSet(); renderSetSide();
});
// a set link pasted into the address bar of an open page
addEventListener('hashchange', () => { const sh = parseShared(location.hash); if (sh) { shared = sh; show('set'); } });

/* ---------- the apps ---------- */
function renderApps() {
  $('#apps-box').innerHTML = `<div class="panel"><div class="qhead"><p class="eyebrow">Where the questions come from</p><h2 class="term">The ChemTrain apps</h2>
      <p class="ask">ChemTrain Daily asks questions built from the cards of these apps. Each one has much more to practise: learning rounds, ranking, flashcards and more.</p></div>
      <div class="apps">${SRC.map(s => { const a = APPS[s]; return `<div class="appcard a-${s}"><h3>${esc(a.name)}</h3><p>${esc(a.about)}</p>
        <p>${a.n} questions here${a.nx === a.n ? ', in problem sets only' : a.nx ? `, ${a.nx} of them in problem sets only` : ''}.</p><div class="links"><a href="${esc(a.page)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open ↗</a><a href="${esc(a.code)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></div></div>`; }).join('')}</div>
      <p class="help">Every app's content is under CC BY 4.0.</p></div>`;
  $('#apps-side').innerHTML = `<div class="bests">${SRC.map(s => `<div><span class="pill a-${s}"><i class="adot"></i>${esc(APPS[s].name)}</span><b>${APPS[s].n}</b></div>`).join('')}
    <div><span class="pill">All apps</span><b>${BANK.length}</b></div></div>`;
}

/* ---------- tabs + keys ---------- */
const VIEWS = ['daily', 'beast', 'set', 'apps'];
let view = 'daily';
function scrollMain() { const top = $('.main').getBoundingClientRect().top; if (top < 0) window.scrollBy({ top: top - 12, behavior: reduceMotion ? 'auto' : 'smooth' }); }
function show(v) {
  view = v;
  for (const name of VIEWS) {
    $('#view-' + name).hidden = name !== v;
    $('#side-' + name).hidden = name !== v;
    $('#tab-' + name).setAttribute('aria-selected', String(name === v));
  }
  if (v === 'daily') { renderDaily(); renderDailySide(); }
  if (v === 'beast') { renderBeast(); renderBeastSide(); }
  if (v === 'set') { renderSet(); renderSetSide(); }
  if (v === 'apps') renderApps();
  scrollMain();
}
document.querySelector('.modes').addEventListener('click', e => { const b = e.target.closest('button[data-view]'); if (b) show(b.dataset.view); });
document.addEventListener('keydown', e => {
  const tag = e.target.tagName;
  if (tag === 'INPUT' || tag === 'TEXTAREA' || e.metaKey || e.ctrlKey || e.altKey) return;
  const k = e.key.toLowerCase();
  const pick = q => { const j = /^[1-4]$/.test(k) ? +k - 1 : (isSite(q) && k.length === 1 ? LETTERS.indexOf(k) : -1); return j >= 0 && j < q.o.length ? j : -1; };
  if (view === 'daily' && dy.started && dyShowing < dy.quiz.length && dyShowing === dy.answers.length) {
    const j = pick(BANK[dy.quiz[dyShowing].i]);
    if (j >= 0) { e.preventDefault(); dailyAnswer(j); return; }
  }
  if (view === 'beast' && bx.phase === 'play' && bx.cur && bx.cur.chosen === null) {
    const j = pick(BANK[bx.cur.i]);
    if (j >= 0) { e.preventDefault(); beastAnswer(j); return; }
  }
  if (view === 'set' && ps && !psDone() && psShowing === ps.answers.length) {
    const j = pick(BANK[ps.items[psShowing].i]);
    if (j >= 0) { e.preventDefault(); psAnswer(j); return; }
  }
  if (e.key === 'Enter' && tag !== 'BUTTON') {
    const b = view === 'daily' ? ($('#dy-next') || $('#dy-start')) : view === 'beast' ? ($('#bx-next') || $('#bx-again') || $('#bx-go'))
      : view === 'set' ? ($('#ps-take') || $('#ps-next') || $('#ps-results') || $('#ps-go')) : null;
    if (b) { e.preventDefault(); b.click(); }
  }
});

/* ---------- boot ---------- */
const start = (location.hash || '').slice(1);
show(shared ? 'set' : VIEWS.includes(start) ? start : 'daily');
</script>
